// Denver Class B: Federal Register document 2026-06762, Docket FAA-2026-2522, final rule effective July 9, 2026, editorial (center point renamed
//   "Point of Origin" at the Denver VOR/DME's coordinates, 39-48-45N 104-39-39W, with the VOR a candidate for decommissioning), full text of Areas A
//   to P read: https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-06762.pdf . Floors used here, computed against that text: over KAPA and along
//   the route south and east of the inner ring, 8,000 (Area E); between the 10 and 15 mile arcs from the 025 radial to the 090 radial, 7,000
//   (Area F); within 10 miles south of the 090 radial and east of Imboden Mile Road, 7,000 (Area D, which holds KCFO; north of that radial within
//   10 miles is Area A, surface to 12,000); 20 to 30 miles between the 025 and 156 radials, 10,000 (Area M); 20 to 30 miles between the 329 and
//   025 radials, 8,000 (Area N); beyond 30 miles none. Radials treated as magnetic (true minus 8). Imboden Road's longitude, about 104-35.0W near
//   KCFO, from OpenStreetMap Nominatim, as were the town centers of Strasburg, Bennett, Roggen, and the Aurora Reservoir.
// Magnetic variation: NOAA NCEI geomagnetic calculator, WMM-2025, for 2026-11-20: KAPA 7.40 E, KGXY 7.36 E, KCFO 7.25 E:
//   https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/geomag-web/calculators/calculateDeclination (the chapter rounds to 7 E).
// Great-circle courses and distances computed from the coordinates above (haversine, initial true course): KAPA to KGXY 53.0 NM at 011 true;
//   KGXY to KCFO 39.5 NM at 174; KCFO to KAPA 19.3 NM at 228. Point to point 111.8 NM. Planned legs: KAPA to BEND (a user waypoint at 39-37.2N
//   104-30.0W, 13.7 NM from the Class B center) 16.4 NM at 079, passing 0.6 NM from the Aurora Reservoir; BEND to Strasburg 9.7 at 046;
//   Strasburg to Roggen 26.2 at 358 (under the 7,000 floor from 2.8 to 10.4 NM past Strasburg); Roggen to KGXY 20.1 at 324; KGXY to Roggen 20.1
//   at 144; Roggen to Strasburg 26.2 at 178; Strasburg to KCFO 9.2 at 290; KCFO to BEND 10.0 at 170; BEND to KAPA 16.4 at 260. Diversion: shower
//   center 12 NM down the Roggen to Strasburg line, drifting toward 140 true at about 16 knots; turn point 19.0 NM from Roggen at 144 (the
//   Greeley to Roggen course held), about 10.5 NM east of the line; turn point to Strasburg 14.8 at 223. Modeled closest approach to the
//   shower's center about 6.5 NM. Every heading and groundspeed solved by the wind triangle with TAS 105 and wind 320 true at 16 knots, worked twice.
// Invented for the story: every METAR, TAF, FB line, ATIS, and controller call; the club planning numbers (105 KTAS, 8.0 gal/hr, 1.0 gallon taxi
//   and runup and 1.0 gallon climb allowance per departure), Biscuit's deviation card, the BEND waypoint name, the snow shower and its exact
//   position; all people's actions. The weather is November-honest: a cold northwest flow with isolated, low-topped snow showers and virga over the
//   plains, not summer thunderstorms. Dates: Tue Nov 10, Thu Nov 12, Mon Nov 16, Thu Nov 19, Sat Nov 21, Sun Nov 22 (MST = UTC minus 7). Russ has
//   Biscuit Nov 13 to 15; Jordan works Friday Nov 13 (a traded shift) and her regular 24s start Nov 11, 14, 17, 20.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 9, part: `Part Two: The Trip`, title: `Cross-Country`, weeks: `November`,
  tasks: [`I.D`,`VI`,`I.E`,`I.C`,`III.B`],
  opening: [
    `Saturday, November 7, ten thirty, the fields by the rec center. The AED was in a white box on the pavilion post, and Jordan found it before she found Ellie. Eighty yards to the near sideline. She walked it once, counting, because she was never going to stop doing that. Then the other way, out to the gate by the parking lot, where the ambulance would have come in. She didn't know which field he'd been on. She hadn't asked. Not today either.`,
    `Claire was in a folding chair at midfield with a thermos and didn't look up. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"You found the box."</span> Jordan said she had. Claire unscrewed the thermos lid, filled it, and held it out, and Jordan took it and stood there with the other parents in the cold.`,
    `Ellie was playing defense, number 4, braids stuffed down the back of her shirt. A kid in orange came up the line with the ball and Ellie went at them like they owed her money. The kid gave it up. Ellie booted it out of bounds, hard, on purpose, and looked over at the sideline to see who'd seen.`,
    `Danny had been right. She was terrifying.`,
    `Tuesday, November 10, 9:20 in the morning, and the endorsement took Abby forty seconds to write. One dual to the practice area and back, steep turns, two stalls, a simulated engine failure over a sugar beet field east of Bennett that Jordan picked early and flew to like she'd been told where it was. On the ramp Abby uncapped the green pen and wrote it standing up, using the wing as a desk.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Practice area. Alone. Still under the shelf, still back by noon, still your card."</span> She capped the pen. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Now the part where you leave."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Leave."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Cross-country. You go somewhere, you land, you come home. Thursday at four. Bring the chart, the plotter, and a pencil you don't love."</span>`,
    `She'd bought the plotter the Saturday before, at the pilot shop by the FBO. Clear plastic, nine dollars, a ruler on one edge and a half moon of degrees in the middle. The man behind the counter had tried to sell her a forty dollar one with a light in it. She'd said her instructor said not the fancy one, and he'd nodded like he'd heard that before, which he probably had.`,
    `The other tool came out of Biscuit's door pocket. She'd found it in April, under the fuel tester and a parking receipt from 2019, and put it back without knowing what it was. Now she knew. An E6B flight computer, the aluminum kind, a slide rule on one side and a clear window over a sliding grid on the other, with D. REYES scratched on the back in capitals somebody had made with a key. On the wind side, in the middle of the frosted window, there was a pencil dot. A little circle around it. His last wind, whatever it had been.`,
    `She didn't erase it. She worked her first practice problem in the margin, around it.`,
    `That night Walt texted. Walt didn't text.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Walt Herrera" time="8:51 PM">Seven and a half months. Cross-country is where students stall. Weather, scheduling, nerves. I'd like to be wrong about this one.</prop>`,
    `She read it twice and decided it was the nicest thing he'd ever said to her, which told her something about Walt, and maybe about her.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: going somewhere`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Leaving the pattern`,
        body: [`Everything so far has been within sight of the tower. Today you learn to leave, and the regulations get specific the minute you do. 61.93 says a student pilot needs its training and endorsements before a solo cross-country, or any flight more than 25 nautical miles from the airport where it started. A solo hop to another airport within 25 miles is handled separately, with its own training and its own endorsement under 61.93(b)(1). Your practice area endorsement doesn't stretch to cover any of that. This one is its own animal.`,
          `Here's what 61.93 wants. First, training from me on a list in paragraph (e): charts with pilotage and dead reckoning and the magnetic compass, performance charts, weather reports and forecasts including estimating visibility in flight, emergency procedures, area departures and arrivals and pattern entries, collision avoidance, wake turbulence, and windshear, the terrain where you'll fly, the equipment in this airplane, radios for navigation and talking, short and soft field and crosswind takeoffs and landings, best angle and best rate climbs, and flying by instruments. Second, two endorsements. One goes in your logbook once, saying you've had the training and you're proficient to fly solo cross-country in a Cessna 172N. The other goes in for each cross-country flight, after I've reviewed your planning, naming the route and the airports and the date.`,
          `That second one has teeth on my side too. Paragraph (d) says I can't let you go unless I've checked your planning is correct, reviewed the current and forecast weather and decided the flight can be finished under VFR, decided you're proficient for it, and checked that your endorsements are current. Your solo endorsement is good for 90 calendar days, so the one from October 20 carries you into January. After that, another one.`,
          `Now the definitions, because the test likes the trap. In everyday language a cross-country is any flight that lands somewhere else. For the hours that count toward your private certificate, 61.1 says a cross-country has to include a landing at a point more than 50 nautical miles, straight line, from where you started. Greeley is 53.0 miles from Centennial, measured from airport to airport. That landing is what makes Sunday count.`,
          `61.109 says you need 5 hours of solo cross-country, and inside that, one long one: 150 nautical miles total distance, full-stop landings at three points, and one leg that's more than 50 nautical miles straight line between takeoff and landing. Centennial, Greeley, the Space Port, and home is 53.0, then 39.5, then 19.3. That's 111.8 point to point. It counts toward the 5 hours. It isn't the long one. With all the doglegs around the Bravo you'll fly about 154 miles, and I'm not going to stand in front of an examiner and argue that three airports a hundred and twelve miles apart make a 150 mile trip. The long one is a different day, and you're going to plan it yourself.`],
        terms: [[`61.93 trigger`,`Any solo cross-country, or any flight more than 25 NM from the origin airport. A solo landing at another airport within 25 NM takes a separate 61.93(b)(1) endorsement.`],[`A.9 endorsement`,`Once, per category and make and model: trained and proficient for solo cross-country (61.93(c)(1) and (2)).`],[`A.10 endorsement`,`Each flight, after the instructor reviews the planning: route, airports, make and model, date (61.93(c)(3)).`],[`Cross-country time (61.1)`,`For private pilot experience, a landing more than 50 NM straight line from the original departure point.`],[`61.109(a)(5)`,`10 hours solo, 5 solo cross-country, one 150 NM trip with three full-stop points and one leg over 50 NM.`]],
        exam: `Know that a student needs an instructor's review and endorsement for each solo cross-country, and that private pilot cross-country time needs a landing more than 50 NM from the departure point.` },
      { h: `Pilotage`,
        body: [`Pilotage is navigating by looking out the window: you match what's on the chart to what's on the ground. It's the oldest method and the one you'll use for the rest of your life, even with a screen in the panel, because the screen can be wrong and the interstate can't.`,
          `The PHAK has good advice on checkpoints. Pick things that are prominent and can be confirmed by something else nearby: a town where a highway crosses a railroad, a reservoir, a big interchange. Better still, pick features that bracket your course, a highway on one side and a river on the other, so if you cross one of them you know which way you've drifted. Never trust one checkpoint by itself. If you miss one, hold your heading and look for the next one. And the line I want you to say out loud: if confused, hold the heading. Turning to go look for something is how people get lost.`,
          `Scale matters. On a sectional, one inch is about 8 statute miles, which is 6.86 nautical. Half an inch off your line is three and a half miles on the ground. The chart also only shows the roads that are easy to see from the air, and new ones show up before the chart catches up. Out east that means a gravel road that isn't on the chart and a paved one that is. Trust the paved one.`,
          `Watch for towers. Antennas out there come in groups with guy wires you can't see, and some of them are taller than you'd believe. The PHAK says don't get within 500 feet above the tallest one. Greeley's Chart Supplement entry lists oil drilling rigs up to 120 feet on and around the airport. Out there they move. The chart doesn't know where they are this week.`],
        terms: [[`Pilotage`,`Navigation by reference to landmarks or checkpoints.`],[`Checkpoints`,`Prominent features confirmable by other features: roads, rivers, railroads, lakes, power lines.`],[`Brackets`,`Features on each side of the course that you shouldn't cross.`],[`Sectional scale`,`1:500,000. One inch is about 8 SM or 6.86 NM.`],[`If confused`,`Hold the heading. Look for the next checkpoint.`]],
        exam: `Expect a question on selecting checkpoints and one on sectional scale. "Hold the heading" is the PHAK's answer when you're unsure.` },
      { h: `Dead reckoning and the wind`,
        body: [`Dead reckoning is the other half. It's navigation by arithmetic: time, airspeed, distance, and direction, corrected for the wind, which gives you a heading to fly and a groundspeed. The heading gets you there and the groundspeed tells you when you should see each checkpoint. You fly both at once. Dead reckoning tells you where you ought to be. Pilotage tells you where you are. The difference between them tells you what the wind is really doing.`,
          `The wind triangle is how you solve it. Three arrows. Your heading and true airspeed. The wind's direction and speed. Add them and you get your track over the ground and your groundspeed. You can draw it with a protractor, which the PHAK walks you through and the test sometimes makes you do. You can solve it on the wind side of an E6B, which is the dot and the grid. Or you can solve it with two sines and a cosine, which is what I'll show you in the drill, because it never lies about which way to turn.`,
          `The working version. Wind angle is the wind direction minus your true course. The wind correction angle is the inverse sine of the wind speed times the sine of the wind angle, divided by true airspeed. A positive answer means turn right, into a wind from the right. Groundspeed is true airspeed times the cosine of the correction angle, minus wind speed times the cosine of the wind angle. A headwind makes that last part big. A tailwind makes it negative, so you add it.`,
          `Where the wind comes from matters. The winds aloft forecast, the FB, gives directions in degrees true, speed in knots, and temperature in Celsius. 3216-06 at 9,000 means from 320 true at 16 knots, minus 6. The plotter measures true courses against the chart's true north lines. So you solve the whole triangle in true and only then convert to magnetic. One more Denver thing: the Aviation Weather Handbook says wind forecasts aren't issued within 1,500 feet of a location's elevation, so the Denver line is blank at 3,000 and 6,000 and starts at 9,000. For a 7,500 foot cruise you use the 9,000 number and you remember it's an estimate.`],
        terms: [[`Dead reckoning`,`Navigation by computation from time, airspeed, distance, and direction, corrected for wind.`],[`Wind triangle`,`Heading and TAS plus wind gives track and groundspeed.`],[`WCA`,`asin(wind speed × sin(wind direction minus true course) ÷ TAS). Positive is right.`],[`Groundspeed`,`TAS × cos(WCA) minus wind speed × cos(wind direction minus true course).`],[`FB winds`,`Direction true, speed knots, temperature C. No wind forecast within 1,500 ft of station elevation.`]],
        exam: `FB winds are true. Sectional courses are true. Solve the wind triangle in true, then convert. That's where most heading questions are won or lost.` },
      { h: `True, magnetic, compass`,
        body: [`The plotter gives you a true course. Biscuit's compass doesn't know what true north is. Getting from one to the other is a chain, and the PHAK prints it in one line: true course, plus or minus the wind correction angle, gives true heading. True heading, plus or minus variation, gives magnetic heading. Magnetic heading, plus or minus deviation, gives compass heading.`,
          `Variation is the angle between true north and magnetic north where you are. The sectional shows it with dashed magenta lines called isogonic lines; the one with zero variation is the agonic line, and it runs well east of here, down through Wisconsin and Illinois and western Tennessee. West of it, magnetic north is east of true. NOAA's model puts Centennial at 7.4 degrees east this month, a hair less at the Space Port, and we round to 7. East is least, west is best: subtract east variation, add west. A true heading of 071 is a magnetic heading of 064.`,
          `Deviation is the airplane's own fault. The engine, the radios, the steel in the seat frames all bend the compass a little, differently on different headings. Somebody swung Biscuit's compass on a compass rose, recorded what it read at every 30 degrees, and wrote a card. It's taped under the compass. It says FOR a magnetic heading, STEER this on the compass. Between the numbers, interpolate in your head. Within a degree is fine.`,
          `The compass has errors you have to fly around, and the test asks. On east or west headings, accelerating makes it swing toward north and slowing down makes it swing toward south. ANDS. Turning toward north in the northern hemisphere, the card lags, so you roll out before it gets there; the PHAK's rule of thumb is 15 degrees plus half your latitude early, so here about 35. Turning toward south it leads, so you go a little past. Undershoot north, overshoot south. And the heading indicator, the gyro, drifts. Friction moves it, and the earth turning underneath it can move it up to 15 degrees an hour. Straight and level, steady speed, reset it to the compass, and do it again every fifteen minutes or so. When the compass is swinging, use the middle of the swing.`],
        terms: [[`The chain`,`TC ± WCA = TH ± V = MH ± D = CH.`],[`Variation`,`Angle between true and magnetic north. About 7 E at KAPA in November 2026. East is least (subtract).`],[`Deviation`,`Compass error from the airplane itself, varies by heading. Read from the deviation card.`],[`ANDS`,`On east or west headings, Accelerate North, Decelerate South.`],[`UNOS`,`Northern hemisphere: Undershoot North, Overshoot South when turning by the magnetic compass.`]],
        exam: `Expect at least one full chain problem. Expect ANDS and the turning error. The trap is adding east variation.` },
      { h: `Time, speed, distance, fuel`,
        body: [`The arithmetic is the same arithmetic you did on that hot day in August, with a clock. Time is distance divided by groundspeed. Distance is groundspeed times time. Fuel is time times burn. Do it in hours and convert, or do it in minutes and keep a 60 in the right place. 26.2 miles at 92 knots is 26.2 divided by 92, times 60, which is 17.1 minutes.`,
          `The fuel rule is 91.151, and it's a floor. You can't begin a flight in an airplane under VFR unless, considering the wind and forecast weather, you have enough fuel to fly to the first point of intended landing and then, at normal cruising speed, fly for 30 more minutes by day or 45 at night. Notice what it doesn't say. It doesn't say anything about the whole trip, only to the first landing. It doesn't say anything about a diversion, except that the wind and weather you knew about count. And it doesn't say 30 minutes is enough. It says less than that is illegal.`,
          `The club plans Biscuit at 105 knots true at 7,500 feet and 8.0 gallons an hour. Those are planning numbers, a little pessimistic on purpose. To each departure add a gallon for start, taxi, and runup and a gallon for the climb, because she burns more climbing and goes slower over the ground doing it. The PHAK says plan for startup, taxi, and the higher burn in the climb, and then add reserve on top. My rule, and the club's, is that you land with an hour. That's 8 gallons. Legal is 4.`,
          `Two more preflight requirements. 91.103 says that before a flight that isn't in the vicinity of an airport you have to become familiar with the weather reports and forecasts, fuel requirements, the alternatives available if you can't finish the planned flight, and any known traffic delays. For any flight, the runway lengths at the airports you'll use and the takeoff and landing distance data. Alternatives is the word I want you to underline. And the clock: weather comes in Zulu, flight plans go in Zulu, and you live in Mountain Standard Time now, UTC minus 7. Your 8:40 departure is fifteen forty Zulu.`],
        terms: [[`Time`,`Distance ÷ groundspeed. Multiply hours by 60 for minutes.`],[`91.151(a)`,`Fuel to the first intended landing plus 30 minutes day or 45 minutes night at normal cruise, considering wind and forecast weather.`],[`Club planning`,`105 KTAS, 8.0 gal/hr, plus 1.0 gal taxi and runup and 1.0 gal climb per departure. Land with an hour.`],[`91.103(a)`,`Away from the airport: weather, fuel, alternatives, known delays.`],[`MST`,`UTC minus 7 after November 1, 2026.`]],
        exam: `Day 30, night 45, to the first point of intended landing. Calculation questions give you a groundspeed and burn and ask for fuel with reserve.` },
      { h: `Altitude, and the shelves`,
        body: [`91.159 is the hemispheric rule. In level cruise more than 3,000 feet above the surface, VFR, below 18,000: on a magnetic course from 0 through 179, fly an odd thousand plus 500, like 7,500 or 9,500. On a magnetic course from 180 through 359, an even thousand plus 500, like 6,500 or 8,500. Magnetic course, not heading. The rule exists so eastbound and westbound traffic at the same altitude are a thousand feet apart.`,
          `Read the first clause again. More than 3,000 feet above the surface. Around here the ground is about 5,000 to 5,500 feet, and the Class B floors sit at 7,000 and 8,000. Under the shelves you're usually less than 3,000 AGL, and the rule doesn't apply. You still pick a sensible number, and out of habit I like the right one for your direction when the floors allow it. But on the test and in the airplane, the floors win, because a Class B clearance is a regulation too and the hemispheric rule has a door built into it.`,
          `Our shelves, from the FAA's own description. The Denver Class B is built out from a center point just south of the airport, in rings at 10, 15, 20, and 30 miles, cut into pie slices by radials and, in places, by roads. Over Centennial and the plains southeast, the floor is 8,000. Northeast of it, between 10 and 15 miles, a slice comes down to 7,000. Farther out, between 20 and 30 miles east, it lifts to 10,000. North, between 20 and 30, it's 8,000. Past 30 miles, there's nothing. Inside 10 miles a lot of it goes to the ground, and the rest starts low. My rule doesn't change: 300 feet below any floor unless a controller says the words cleared into the Bravo. Under 8,000, that's 7,700. Under 7,000, it's 6,700.`,
          `The Space Port is the interesting one. It's inside the 10 mile ring, but it sits in a notch, south of a line running east from the center and east of a county road, Imboden, that the FAA uses as a boundary, where the floor is 7,000 instead of the ground. North of that line, inside 10 miles, the Bravo goes to the surface. So you don't come into the Space Port from the north. You come in from the east, under the 7,000, and you leave to the south or east the same way.`,
          `And since someone always asks: a 172N runs out of climb somewhere around 14,000 feet on a good day, and you're not going to find out which day.`],
        terms: [[`91.159`,`More than 3,000 ft AGL: magnetic course 0 to 179, odd thousands + 500; 180 to 359, even thousands + 500.`],[`Below 3,000 AGL`,`The hemispheric rule doesn't apply. Under the Denver shelves you're often there.`],[`Abby's cap`,`300 ft below any Class B floor unless cleared: 7,700 under 80, 6,700 under 70.`],[`KCFO notch`,`Floor 7,000 east of Imboden Road and south of the 090 radial. North of it, inside 10 miles, the Bravo starts at the surface.`]],
        exam: `Hemispheric altitudes go by magnetic course and only apply above 3,000 ft AGL. Know which number is odd plus 500.` },
      { h: `The VOR`,
        body: [`The VOR is a radio station on the ground that sends out a signal you can turn into a direction. It works line of sight, so the higher you are the farther you'll pick it up. It's going away slowly. The FAA is shutting down VORs it doesn't need, and the Denver VOR is on that list; this year they even rewrote the Class B's description so its center is a point on the map instead of that station. The test still asks about VORs, Biscuit still has one, and on a day the GPS quits you'll be glad.`,
          `A radial is a magnetic bearing from the station. The 030 radial is the line running out from the station toward the northeast, 030 degrees magnetic. On Biscuit's indicator you turn the OBS, the knob, to pick a course. The needle shows whether that course is left or right of you, and the TO or FROM flag says whether flying that course takes you toward the station or away from it. Centered needle, 030 under the index, FROM flag: you're on the 030 radial, northeast of the station. Same needle, same 030, TO flag: you're on the 210 radial, southwest of it, and flying 030 takes you to it. The indicator doesn't care which way the airplane is pointed. Set the course you want to fly and the needle reads the right way. Set the reciprocal and you get reverse sensing, where chasing the needle drives you away from it.`,
          `Identify it every time. The AIM says the only positive way to identify a VOR is its Morse code identifier or its recorded voice identifier, and not a controller saying its name. DEN is dash dot dot, dot, dash dot. During maintenance a station may send T E S T or nothing at all, and then you don't use it.`,
          `Accuracy is excellent, generally within a degree. The IFR check rules are worth knowing for the test: a ground check within plus or minus 4 degrees, an airborne check within plus or minus 6, and with two receivers on the same station, no more than 4 degrees apart. A degree off the radial is about a mile wide at 60 miles out and half a mile at 30. And the time-distance trick: turn so the station is off your wingtip, time how long it takes the bearing to change 10 degrees, and the seconds divided by the degrees give you minutes to the station. Ninety seconds for 10 degrees is nine minutes.`],
        terms: [[`Radial`,`A magnetic bearing from the station.`],[`TO/FROM`,`Whether the selected course takes you toward or away from the station.`],[`Identification`,`Positive only by Morse code or the recorded voice ID.`],[`VOR checks`,`Ground ±4°, airborne ±6°, dual receivers within 4° of each other.`],[`Time to station`,`Seconds for the bearing change ÷ degrees of change = minutes.`]],
        exam: `Radial means from. The indicator ignores heading. Morse code is the positive ID. Expect a time-to-station calculation.` },
      { h: `GPS`,
        body: [`The panel GPS is the best navigation tool you'll ever have in this airplane, and the AIM and the PHAK both want you to treat it as one tool among several. The AIM says VFR pilots should integrate GPS with other electronic navigation when they can, and with pilotage and dead reckoning. That's the sentence the checkride examiner will ask you to explain.`,
          `Here's why. A VFR panel unit or a handheld has no RAIM alerting. RAIM is the receiver checking itself, making sure it has enough good satellites to trust its own answer. Without it, the box can lose satellites or work out a bad position and not tell you. The fix is a cross-check. If the GPS and the interstate disagree, believe the interstate and find out why.`,
          `The database is the second thing. There's no legal requirement to keep a VFR database current, and the AIM recommends you do, and says not to use a moving map with an old database around critical airspace. Out here critical airspace means everything with a Bravo floor over it. Ours is current. Check the date on the start page anyway, because checking is the habit.`,
          `Third, the head-down problem. The PHAK says if you're going to use your own waypoints, enter them before you take off, and don't build routes in flight. Know the buttons on the ground. Every minute your eyes are in the box is a minute nobody's looking for the Bonanza.`],
        terms: [[`RAIM`,`Receiver Autonomous Integrity Monitoring. VFR units generally don't alert you to a bad position.`],[`Integrate`,`AIM: use GPS together with pilotage, dead reckoning, and other navigation.`],[`Database`,`Not required to be current for VFR, but recommended; don't trust an old one near critical airspace.`],[`User waypoints`,`Enter before flight. Verify against a current chart.`]],
        exam: `Know what RAIM does and that most VFR receivers lack the alert. The safe answer is always a cross-check.` },
      { h: `Flight following`,
        body: [`You've used it. Now the fine print. The AIM calls it radar traffic information service: the controller tells you about radar targets that might come close enough to your route to deserve your attention. It's provided when the controller thinks they can, which depends on radar limits, traffic, workload, and how busy the frequency is, and the AIM says their reason for saying no isn't subject to question. In practice around Denver they almost always say yes.`,
          `What it isn't. It doesn't relieve you of seeing and avoiding other airplanes. It doesn't include vectors away from traffic unless you ask. It isn't a Class B clearance, and radar contact isn't permission to climb. And while you have it, you monitor that frequency all the time, because if you go silent the controller has to wonder whether you've lost your radio or worse. When you don't want it anymore, tell them before you change frequencies, and go back to 1200 unless they've done it for you.`,
          `The request is the same five things you've practiced: who you are, where you are, your altitude, what you want, and where you're going. At Greeley there's no tower, so near the airport Approach will terminate radar service and you'll switch to the airport's common frequency. You talk to the airplanes yourself there.`],
        terms: [[`Radar traffic information`,`Advisories on targets near your route, provided workload permitting.`],[`Not included`,`Relief from see and avoid. Vectors, unless requested. Clearance into Class B.`],[`Monitor`,`Stay on the assigned frequency. Tell the controller before leaving it.`]],
        exam: `Flight following is workload permitting and never relieves you of see and avoid. That's most of what the test asks.` },
      { h: `Flight plans`,
        body: [`A VFR flight plan is your search and rescue insurance. Inside the continental U.S. it's not normally required. The AIM strongly recommends one anyway. You file it with Flight Service, you open it yourself after takeoff, and you close it yourself when you're down. Towers don't open VFR flight plans and they don't close them, because they don't know who's on one. If you don't close it, or update it, within half an hour after your ETA, search and rescue starts. People will get in cars. They'll call your mother.`,
          `91.153 lists what goes in it: your aircraft identification, the type, your name and address, departure point and proposed time, the route, cruising altitude, true airspeed, your first intended landing and the estimated time to get there, fuel on board in hours, the number of people aboard, and anything else ATC needs. The AIM says filing is now done on the international form, FAA Form 7233-4, and the apps fill it in for you.`,
          `For a trip with stops, file a separate flight plan for each leg. That's straight out of the AIM. A proposal is kept for about two hours after your proposed departure time, so if you're late by more than that, file again. You can activate one with an assumed departure time, but the AIM warns that if you don't update it, search and rescue will run off that time. Easier to just open it on the radio after takeoff and close it on the phone after you shut down.`,
          `One more item the ACS lists under cross-country planning: interception. If a military aircraft ever pulls up next to you, follow its instructions and read AIM chapter 5, section 6 before then. It won't happen to you over Greeley. Know where it lives anyway.`],
        terms: [[`VFR flight plan`,`Not normally required; strongly recommended. Activated and closed by the pilot with Flight Service.`],[`Towers`,`Don't automatically activate or close VFR flight plans.`],[`Search and rescue`,`Starts if a flight plan isn't closed or updated within 1/2 hour after ETA.`],[`91.153`,`ID, type, pilot, departure point and time, route, altitude, TAS, first landing and ETE, fuel in hours, persons aboard.`],[`Stopovers`,`A separate flight plan for each leg.`]],
        exam: `Thirty minutes after ETA is the search and rescue trigger. Fuel on a flight plan goes in hours, not gallons.` },
      { h: `Lost`,
        body: [`Getting lost isn't the emergency. Staying lost while the fuel goes down is. The PHAK's first step, if you can't see a town, is to climb, minding traffic and weather, because altitude lets you see farther, pick up navigation signals farther, and show up on radar farther. Under our shelves you can't climb much, so the shelf is part of the answer too: you know you're under something, and that narrows it down.`,
          `Then look for the big stuff. An interstate. A railroad running next to it. A reservoir. The name on a water tower if you're low enough, which you shouldn't need to be. Tune and identify a VOR and find what radial you're on; two radials from two stations cross at your position. If you have GPS and it's working, use it, and cross-check it.`,
          `And talk. Approach can find you on radar and give you vectors. If you can't raise anybody, 121.5 is the emergency frequency, and somebody's always listening. If it's getting serious, say so, declare it, and squawk 7700. The common mnemonic is climb, communicate, confess, comply. The confess part is the hard one for people like you. Do it early. Controllers have heard worse.`],
        terms: [[`First step (PHAK)`,`If you can't see a town, climb, mindful of traffic and weather.`],[`Fix by VOR`,`Two radials from two stations cross at your position.`],[`Assistance`,`Radar vectors, 121.5, and 7700 if the situation is threatening.`]],
        exam: `Climb first, then use every resource. Lost procedure questions reward asking for help early.` },
      { h: `Diverting, and November`,
        body: [`A diversion is when you decide not to go where you planned. The PHAK lists the reasons: unpredicted weather, a malfunction, or poor planning, which we're going to avoid. It says risk management becomes the priority, and it names the case you'll meet most: a pre-planned or in-flight diversion around hazardous weather. Before any cross-country, look along the route for airports and places to land, and for navigation aids you could use.`,
          `In the air, take shortcuts. You don't plot on a chart in a bumpy cockpit. Pick the alternate, estimate the course from the chart or a VOR rose, and if there's time, start the turn over something you can recognize, so you know exactly where the new leg began. In a real emergency, turn first and figure later. Then note the time, use the nearest winds to work a heading and a groundspeed, and from those an arrival time and the fuel it costs. Fly the airplane while you do it.`,
          `For small corrections there's the 60 to 1 rule. One degree off course is about one mile off for every 60 miles flown. If you're 1 mile off after 10 miles, your heading was off 6 degrees. Turn 6 to stop the drift, plus enough to get back by the next checkpoint: 1 mile in 16 more is about 4 degrees. Ten degrees total, then back to 6 once you're on the line.`,
          `Now the weather you'll actually see. In November the big July thunderstorms are gone. What we get instead behind a cold front is cold air with a little instability in it, and that builds snow showers over the plains. Small ones, sometimes only a few miles across, with flat dark bases and a gray curtain under them, and virga, snow that evaporates before it reaches the ground, hanging off the edges. They look soft. Inside one the visibility goes to nothing in about a second and the ground disappears, which for a VFR pilot is the whole emergency. You don't go under them, through them, or close to them. You give them miles, and you go around the side that has clear air and a way home.`,
          `The days are short now too. Sunset's around half past four. Plan to be tied down with daylight left over.`],
        terms: [[`Diversion`,`Change of destination or route for weather, a malfunction, or a planning problem. Plan alternates before flight.`],[`Diversion technique`,`Start over a recognizable point, note the time, compute heading, GS, ETA, and fuel.`],[`60 to 1 rule`,`1° off ≈ 1 NM off per 60 NM flown.`],[`Snow showers`,`Low-topped, small, with virga. Zero visibility inside. Give them miles.`]],
        exam: `For diversion questions: turn promptly toward the alternate, then compute. For off-course questions, use the 60 to 1 rule.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f9s1`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.D`, title:`A straight line`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, November 12, four in the afternoon, the briefing room. Jordan had come off a shift at seven that morning and slept until two, and she still had the station's coffee taste in her mouth. Abby had the Denver sectional unfolded across both tables with a coffee mug on each corner, the way you'd pin down a patient who didn't want an IV.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Centennial to Greeley. Draw it."</span>`,`Jordan laid the new plotter from the little airport symbol at Centennial to the one east of Greeley and ran a pencil down the edge. Fifty three miles. It went north, a little east, and straight through the solid blue rings around Denver International. Not near them. Through the middle of the innermost one, where the number in the blue said the Bravo came all the way down to the ground.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Pretty line,"</span> Abby said. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your trip. Where does it go?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep the straight line, and call Denver Approach for a Bravo clearance ten miles out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You don't have a 61.95 endorsement, and I'm not giving you one so you can save twenty miles. And say they tell you to remain clear at twelve miles out. What's the plan then?"</span> Jordan looks at the line. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Right. You make one up in the air, alone, with a Bravo in front of you. Plan the trip you can fly without a yes."</span> She hands Jordan the eraser.`,
          why:`A student can't solo in Class B without 61.95 training and an endorsement, and a plan that depends on a clearance you may not get leaves you inventing the backup in flight. Plan a route that works without one.`},
        {t:`Bend it east around the inner rings, and list every shelf floor along it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She erases the line and draws it again in pieces. East first, out over the Aurora Reservoir, then a turn northeast to Strasburg on I-70, then due north across open country to Roggen on I-76, then northwest into Greeley. Seventy two miles instead of fifty three. Next to each piece she writes the floor in a column, the way she'd write vitals: 80, 80, then 70 for a stretch, 80, 100, 80, nothing. Abby taps the 70 with her pencil. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's the one people miss. Everybody knows Centennial's 80. Nobody looks up out there."</span> She checks the turn east of the reservoir. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There's nothing on the ground at that corner, so make it a GPS point. Build it Sunday morning at the kitchen table, not on the climbout."</span> Jordan writes BEND next to it. Abby considers this. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Fine. It's a bend."</span>`,
          why:`Route planning starts with airspace (PA.I.D.K1). Going around the inner rings keeps the flight under the shelves without needing a clearance, and listing every floor along the route is how you avoid the one that drops lower than you expect.`},
        {t:`Bend it west around the rings instead, past downtown and Rocky Mountain Metro.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Legal. Look what's over there."</span> She points with the pencil: the Class D at Rocky Mountain Metro, the towers downtown, the shelves stacked closer together, the foothills rising under all of it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"East is boring. Flat, empty, one shelf at a time. On your first solo cross-country, boring is the point."</span> Jordan draws it again on the east side.`,
          why:`The west side can be flown under the shelves, but it adds another Class D, more traffic, more shelf changes, and rising terrain. The ACS asks you to weigh airspace and terrain when choosing a route. The simpler route around the east has fewer hazards.`},
        {t:`Keep the straight line, and hold 6,000 feet under the shelf floors the whole way.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Abby puts her finger on the innermost ring. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Read the number."</span> Jordan reads it. The Bravo there starts at the surface. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There's no under. That's Denver International's front yard. You'd be landing on their runway without asking."</span>`,
          why:`The innermost part of the Denver Class B extends from the surface, so there's no altitude under it. Entering requires a clearance and, for a student solo, a 61.95 endorsement. Check the floor of every piece before assuming you can go under.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s2`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`VI`, title:`East is least`, q:`Ellie's compass and the map disagree by seven degrees. How should Jordan convert her heading?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`That evening she took the nav log to Claire's, because Claire had texted <b>tamales, come eat them or I'll cry</b>, and because Ellie had math homework and wanted company.`,`Ellie's homework was angles. Jordan's homework was angles. They sat across from each other at the kitchen table with the overhead light on, and after twenty minutes Ellie looked up from a protractor and asked where north was.`,`Jordan pointed out the window, at the Johnsons' garage. Ellie produced a little plastic compass from a cereal box and set it on the table. The needle settled a bit to the right of the garage.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Your north and my north are different."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Mine's the map's. Yours is the magnet's. Around here they're about seven degrees apart."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"So who's right?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Both of us. You just have to know how far apart."</span> Ellie found that unsatisfying and said so.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Biscuit's compass card, taped under the compass">FOR&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;N&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;30&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;60&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;E&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;120&nbsp;&nbsp;150<br>STEER&nbsp;001&nbsp;&nbsp;031&nbsp;&nbsp;062&nbsp;&nbsp;092&nbsp;&nbsp;121&nbsp;&nbsp;150<br><br>FOR&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;S&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;210&nbsp;&nbsp;240&nbsp;&nbsp;W&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;300&nbsp;&nbsp;330<br>STEER&nbsp;178&nbsp;&nbsp;208&nbsp;&nbsp;238&nbsp;&nbsp;268&nbsp;&nbsp;299&nbsp;&nbsp;330<br><br>RADIOS ON. SWUNG 3/14/24, M. TRAN</prop>`,`The first row of her log: Centennial to BEND, true course 079 off the plotter. Wind at 9,000 forecast from 320 true at 16 knots. Her wind correction angle came out 8 degrees left, which made the true heading 071. Variation 7 east. Ellie watched her pencil hover.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take the 071 true heading and subtract the 7 east: 064 magnetic.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`<b>MH 064.</b> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"East is least,"</span> she says, in Abby's exact sing song, and Ellie fires back <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"West is best"</span> before anybody asks her. So somebody taught her that. Jordan can guess who. Then the card. 064 is closest to 060, and 060 says steer 062, so two more. <b>CH 066.</b> Ellie leans across the tamales and reads the card upside down. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"So the airplane has its own north too?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Sort of. Two degrees off."</span> Ellie squints at it. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Because it's old?"</span> Jordan, still writing: <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Because it's full of wires and an engine."</span> Ellie decides this is Biscuit's fault and not Jordan's, which is pretty generous for a nine year old, and goes back to her protractor.`,
          why:`PHAK chapter 16: TC ± WCA = TH ± V = MH ± D = CH. East variation is subtracted, so a true heading of 071 with 7° E is a magnetic heading of 064. Deviation from the card then gives the compass heading.`},
        {t:`Add the 7 east to the 071 true heading for 078 magnetic.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She writes 078 and stops. Across the table Ellie's kicking the chair leg and chanting something under her breath, over and over, like a jump rope song. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"East is least."</span> Jordan puts the pencil down. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Where'd you get that?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"It's on the fridge. Daddy wrote it."</span> And there it is, on a yellow sticky note gone soft at the corners, stuck under a dentist magnet shaped like a tooth, between a pizza coupon and the school lunch menu for October. Jordan looks at it for maybe ten seconds. Then she erases 078, hard enough to rough up the paper, and writes 064.`,
          why:`East variation is subtracted, not added. Adding it would put the airplane 14 degrees off the planned track, which over 16 miles is roughly 4 miles of error. "East is least, west is best."`},
        {t:`Subtract the 7 east from the 079 true course for 072, and work the wind in flight.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Seventy two."</span> Then she pictures doing the wind part over Aurora, climbing through 7,000, tower in one ear and a Cherokee out there somewhere she hasn't found yet. No thanks. She puts it back in order, true course to true heading first, and gets 064. On the ground the math is free. In the air it costs you a look outside.`,
          why:`Variation was applied correctly, but leaving out the wind correction gives a magnetic course, not a heading. Over the leg an 8 degree crosswind correction is worth about 2 miles. Solve the whole chain on the ground.`},
        {t:`Skip the conversion, fly the GPS course line, and keep its needle centered.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Ellie's been listening the whole time, it turns out. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"What if it breaks?"</span> Jordan opens her mouth, then shuts it. Ellie, helpfully: <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Mom's car thing broke on the way to Grandma's and Mom had to call Grandma to find Grandma's."</span> Which, okay. Hard to argue with that. Jordan does the math, every bit of it, in pencil, and Ellie watches to make sure.`,
          why:`The AIM says VFR pilots should integrate GPS with pilotage and dead reckoning, and VFR units can fail or give bad positions with no warning. The compass heading is the backup you need when the screen goes blank.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s3`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.D`, title:`Seven thousand`, q:`What altitude should Jordan plan between Strasburg and Roggen?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, November 16. She'd flown the practice area alone that morning, 1.2 on the Hobbs, her first time out of sight of the tower by herself, and had spent most of it looking at the ground and saying the names of things out loud. Now it was the kitchen table and the altitude column.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Kneeboard, leg one, the floors">KAPA TO BEND: B 80. NOTHING ABOVE 7,700.<br>BEND TO STRASBURG: B 80.<br>STRASBURG TO ROGGEN: 80 FOR 3 MI. THEN 70 FOR ABOUT 7. THEN 80. THEN 100. THEN 80 AGAIN AT THE INTERSTATE.<br>ROGGEN TO KGXY: 80 FOR ABOUT 10. THEN NOTHING.<br>GROUND: ABOUT 5,000 TO 5,500 UNDER ALL OF IT. KGXY FIELD 4,697.</prop>`,`The Strasburg to Roggen leg was the long one, 26 miles due north across country with no towns to speak of, and its magnetic course was 351. Westbound. She knew the rule for westbound. She also knew the column.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`8,500, the westbound altitude for a 351 magnetic course.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She texts the plan to Abby, who calls instead of texting back. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"What's the floor at Strasburg?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Eighty."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And you'd be at eighty five. That's the Bravo. The hemispheric rule starts at three thousand above the ground and it has an 'unless' in it. The shelf doesn't."</span> Jordan redoes the column: 7,500, 6,700 under the 70, back to 7,500.`,
          why:`91.159 applies only above 3,000 AGL and never authorizes entering Class B. At 8,500 she'd be inside the 8,000 shelf without a clearance. Under the Denver shelves the floors decide the altitude.`},
        {t:`7,500 for the whole leg, under the 8,000 floor that covers most of the east side.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Abby reads the log on the phone that night. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Read me line three of your own kneeboard."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Seventy for about seven."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"So for seven miles you'd be five hundred feet inside the Bravo. You wrote it down and then didn't use it. That's the one people miss. I told you that."</span> Jordan adds 6,700 for the middle of the leg.`,
          why:`The 7,000 shelf northeast of the airport sits right on the route. At 7,500 she'd be in Class B for several miles without a clearance. Every floor on the route matters, not only the familiar one.`},
        {t:`Step down from 7,500 to 6,700 under the 7,000 shelf.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She works it: 7,500 over ground at about 5,400 near Strasburg is 2,100 feet above it, under 3,000, so 91.159 doesn't apply, and she picks her numbers by the shelves. 6,700 is 300 under the 70. She draws a small step down in the altitude column at 3 miles and a step up at 10. Abby reads it that night and sends back one line. <b>Good. Past Greeley, if you're ever above 3,000 AGL westbound, it's 8,500. Not today.</b>`,
          why:`91.159 applies in level cruise more than 3,000 feet above the surface. Under the Denver shelves the terrain puts her below that, so the Class B floors set the altitude, with Abby's 300 foot margin below each one.`},
        {t:`6,500 for the whole leg, under every floor in her column.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Legal. Low for no reason, though."</span> Over ground at 5,400 that's 1,100 feet. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Engine quits at 1,100 feet, you get about a minute and a half of choices. At 7,500 you get twice that. Altitude's free until the shelf says it isn't."</span> Jordan changes it to 7,500 with the step down under the 70.`,
          why:`6,500 avoids every floor but gives away altitude, and with it glide distance and options in an engine failure, for 26 miles. The ACS asks you to select altitude considering terrain, glide distance, and airspace together.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s4`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`VI`, title:`Sticky note`, q:`After five minutes of Abby's turns under the hood, the GPS is covered. What should Jordan do first?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, November 19, the dual cross-country, Abby in the right seat with a thermos and a pack of yellow sticky notes. They flew the whole route the way Jordan had planned it: BEND, Strasburg, the step down under the 70, and for twenty minutes it was very calm and very flat.`,`Somewhere north of I-70 Abby said <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Hood,"</span> and Jordan put the foggles on, and for five minutes she flew whatever Abby said. Left turns. A climb. A long right turn. A descent. She lost count of the turns on purpose because Abby told her to.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I have the airplane. Hood off."</span> When Jordan looked up, there was a sticky note over the GPS screen with a face drawn on it, a frown. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"GPS just died. You're lost. Your airplane."</span>`,`Below: fields, brown and white, a dirt road, a center pivot like a long metal arm laid out in a circle. 6,600 on the altimeter. Nothing she recognized.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Hold her heading and match the biggest landmark in sight to the chart.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She rolls wings level on 360 and holds it, the PHAK's line running in her head. Ahead, maybe five miles, a straight gray line angles across her path toward the east-northeast with a thinner line beside it, an interstate and a railroad running together, which on this chart can only be I-76. She tunes the Denver VOR and listens: dash dot dot, dot, dash dot. Turns the OBS until the needle centers with a FROM flag. 028. She finds the 028 radial on the chart, follows it out from the station to where it crosses south of I-76. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"About twenty miles northeast of the Denver VOR, south of the interstate. Roggen's just ahead, where we'll meet the interstate."</span> Abby peels off the sticky note. The GPS agrees within a mile. Abby writes something on her kneeboard and doesn't say what.`,
          why:`PHAK lost procedures: hold the heading, use prominent features, and fix your position with VOR radials or GPS, then communicate if needed. Identifying the VOR by Morse code is the AIM's positive identification.`},
        {t:`Call Denver Approach, give her altitude, and ask them for her position.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Not wrong. On a real day, if you're worried, call early. Today, try first, then call. You have a chart, a compass, a VOR, and a lot of visibility."</span> Jordan looks out, finds the interstate, tunes the VOR, and figures it out in two minutes. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"See. Now you can tell them where you are instead of asking."</span>`,
          why:`Communicating is part of the lost procedure, and radar help is available. But with good visibility and working navigation equipment, first using pilotage and the VOR builds the skill and gives ATC a better starting point.`},
        {t:`Descend to read the name off the nearest water tower.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She starts down. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Stop. Why down?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"To read the tower."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Down is less radio, less radar, less ground to look at, and more things like that tower to hit. The book says climb."</span> She points up, at the shelf, then out, at the interstate. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Climb to what the shelf allows and look farther."</span>`,
          why:`The PHAK's first lost step, if no town is visible, is to climb, mindful of traffic and weather, which extends visibility, radio range, and radar coverage. Descending to read signs reduces margins and options.`},
        {t:`Peel off the note and check her position on the GPS.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"On a real flight, sure. And then you check it against something, because that box has no way to tell you it's wrong. Today it's broken. Find us."</span> The note stays where it is.`,
          why:`Using GPS to find your position is legitimate. But VFR receivers lack RAIM alerting, so the AIM says to cross-check with pilotage and other navigation. The skill being trained is finding your position when the GPS isn't available.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s5`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.B`, title:`Thirty five preferred`, q:`Greeley's frequency is quiet, the wind's calm, and runway 35 is preferred. How should Jordan join the pattern?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Twelve miles southeast of Greeley, Approach let them go. Radar service terminated, squawk VFR, frequency change approved. Jordan switched to Greeley's common frequency and listened, and nobody said anything for a long time, which at Centennial never happened.`,`The AWOS said wind calm, visibility 10, few clouds at 12,000, altimeter three zero two one. On her kneeboard she'd copied two lines from the Chart Supplement the night before: <b>RWY 35 PREFERRED WHEN WIND LESS THAN 5 KTS. OIL DRILLING RIGS UP TO 120 FT ON AND INVOF ARPT.</b> And the pattern altitude, 5,500 rounded, 800 feet above the field.`,`Greeley was spread across the windshield, the city on the left and the airport on its east side, two long runways in an L. Abby sat with her hands in her lap.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Line up straight in for runway 28, the closest one to her.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She turns toward 28. At four miles a high wing pops out of the haze over the field, a Skyhawk on a left downwind for 35, and a voice comes on the frequency for the first time: <span class="said" data-who="man">"Greeley traffic, Skyhawk on left downwind three five, full stop."</span> They'd have met at the intersection. Abby, quietly: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Quiet doesn't mean empty. Some people forget the radio. Some don't have one."</span>`,
          why:`At a nontowered airport, aircraft without radios are legal and some pilots don't announce. Fly the preferred runway and a standard pattern entry so other traffic knows where to look for you.`},
        {t:`Cross over well above the pattern, then join on the 45 for left downwind 35.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Greeley traffic, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, ten miles southeast, inbound for left traffic runway three five, Greeley."</span> She crosses the field at 6,500, well above anything in the pattern, flies out to the west, descends to 5,500 away from the downwind, and turns back in on a 45. A Skyhawk comes up on the frequency, already on downwind. She sees it, calls it, follows it. Base, final, ten thousand feet of runway in front of her. Abby, as the mains touch: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You could land three times on this one. Don't."</span>`,
          why:`At nontowered airports the standard entry is the 45 to the downwind at pattern altitude, with position reports on the common frequency. Crossing well above the pattern and descending outside it keeps you clear of traffic already there.`},
        {t:`Announce her position, and fly a straight-in approach to runway 35 from the south.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She calls a six mile final. A Skyhawk, already on the downwind and not expecting her, says <span class="said" data-who="man">"Uh, Skyhawk extending downwind for the straight-in traffic,"</span> in the voice of somebody being polite. Abby: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Legal. Not neighborly. The 45 lets everybody see you coming."</span>`,
          why:`Straight-in approaches aren't prohibited, but the recommended practice at nontowered airports is to enter on the 45 to downwind, which makes you predictable to traffic in the pattern. A straight-in can cut in front of pilots already established.`},
        {t:`Join the left downwind for 35 at 6,500, a thousand feet above pattern altitude.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Then everybody at pattern altitude has you right over their heads where they can't see you, and you have to come down through them to land."</span> Jordan descends to 5,500 outside the pattern and re-enters on the 45.`,
          why:`Fly the published pattern altitude so traffic is where other pilots expect it. Descending into the pattern from above puts you in a blind spot for high-wing airplanes below and conflicts with their final.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s6`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.D`, title:`One leg at a time`, q:`Jordan's day has three legs and two stops. How should she use VFR flight plans?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, November 21, 8 PM. She'd worked Friday into Saturday, a quiet one for once, and slept until two. Now the kitchen table again, with the finished log, a mug of tea gone cold, and the chart folded to the Denver panel so the route ran up the middle of the fold.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Nav log, N4791E, Sunday 11/22. TAS 105. Wind 320/16 (FB 9000). Var 7E. Dev from nearest card heading."><code>LEG 1. KAPA TO KGXY<br>CHECKPOINT&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;TC&nbsp;&nbsp;WCA&nbsp;TH&nbsp;&nbsp;MH&nbsp;&nbsp;DEV&nbsp;CH&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;DIST&nbsp;GS&nbsp;&nbsp;ETE&nbsp;FUEL&nbsp;ALT<br>BEND (RES.)&nbsp;&nbsp;079&nbsp;-8&nbsp;&nbsp;071&nbsp;064&nbsp;+2&nbsp;&nbsp;066&nbsp;16.4&nbsp;112&nbsp;9&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1.2&nbsp;&nbsp;7500<br>STRASBURG&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;046&nbsp;-9&nbsp;&nbsp;037&nbsp;030&nbsp;+1&nbsp;&nbsp;031&nbsp;&nbsp;9.7&nbsp;103&nbsp;6&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0.8&nbsp;&nbsp;7500<br>ROGGEN&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;358&nbsp;-5&nbsp;&nbsp;353&nbsp;346&nbsp;+1&nbsp;&nbsp;347&nbsp;26.2&nbsp;&nbsp;92&nbsp;17&nbsp;&nbsp;2.3&nbsp;&nbsp;7500/6700/7500<br>KGXY&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;324&nbsp;-1&nbsp;&nbsp;323&nbsp;316&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;316&nbsp;20.1&nbsp;&nbsp;89&nbsp;14&nbsp;&nbsp;1.8&nbsp;&nbsp;7500<br>TOTAL 72.4 NM, 46 MIN. CRUISE 6.1 + TAXI 1.0 + CLIMB 1.0 = 8.1 GAL<br><br>LEG 2. KGXY TO KCFO<br>ROGGEN&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;144&nbsp;+1&nbsp;&nbsp;145&nbsp;138&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;138&nbsp;20.1&nbsp;121&nbsp;10&nbsp;&nbsp;1.3&nbsp;&nbsp;7500<br>STRASBURG&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;178&nbsp;+5&nbsp;&nbsp;183&nbsp;176&nbsp;-2&nbsp;&nbsp;174&nbsp;26.2&nbsp;117&nbsp;13&nbsp;&nbsp;1.8&nbsp;&nbsp;7500/6700/7500<br>KCFO&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;290&nbsp;+4&nbsp;&nbsp;294&nbsp;287&nbsp;-1&nbsp;&nbsp;286&nbsp;&nbsp;9.2&nbsp;&nbsp;91&nbsp;6&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0.8&nbsp;&nbsp;DESC 6500 (B 70)<br>TOTAL 55.5 NM, 29 MIN. CRUISE 3.9 + 2.0 = 5.9 GAL<br>PLAN B: EAST AROUND ANYTHING THAT BUILDS. NEVER WEST (BRAVO). OR BACK TO KGXY.<br><br>LEG 3. KCFO TO KAPA<br>BEND&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;170&nbsp;+4&nbsp;&nbsp;174&nbsp;167&nbsp;-2&nbsp;&nbsp;165&nbsp;10.0&nbsp;119&nbsp;5&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0.7&nbsp;&nbsp;6500 (B 70)<br>KAPA&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;260&nbsp;+8&nbsp;&nbsp;268&nbsp;261&nbsp;-2&nbsp;&nbsp;259&nbsp;16.4&nbsp;&nbsp;96&nbsp;10&nbsp;&nbsp;1.4&nbsp;&nbsp;7500<br>TOTAL 26.4 NM, 15 MIN. CRUISE 2.1 + 2.0 = 4.1 GAL<br><br>DAY: 154.3 NM FLOWN, 111.8 POINT TO POINT. FUEL 18.1 GAL. LAND WITH AN HOUR (8.0).</code></prop>`,`<prop type="doc" title="Flight plan, leg one, the way the app laid it out">AIRCRAFT: N4791E. C172.<br>PILOT: JORDAN E. REYES, AURORA CO.<br>DEPARTURE: KAPA, 1540Z.<br>ROUTE: BEND (USER WAYPOINT 39 37.2N 104 30.0W), STRASBURG, ROGGEN.<br>CRUISING ALTITUDE: 7,500 (6,700 NORTH OF STRASBURG UNDER CLASS B 7,000). TRUE AIRSPEED: 105 KTS.<br>DESTINATION: KGXY. ESTIMATED TIME EN ROUTE: 0 HR 53 MIN.<br>FUEL ON BOARD: 3 HR 00 MIN.<br>PERSONS ON BOARD: 1. AIRCRAFT COLOR: WHITE, MAROON STRIPE.<br>REMARKS: STUDENT PILOT. SOLO CROSS-COUNTRY.</prop>`,`The app had a button at the bottom that said FILE. She'd filled it in twice to be sure. Under it, smaller, another one: ADD STOP.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`File one plan for the whole day with the stops in the remarks, and let the tower close it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She texts Abby a screenshot. The phone rings almost before it's sent. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Towers don't close VFR flight plans. They don't even know you're on one. Half an hour after your ETA, somebody starts calling Greeley asking where you are, and you're sitting at the Space Port eating a granola bar."</span> Jordan deletes it and files three.`,
          why:`AIM 5-1-5 and 5-1-14: towers don't activate or close VFR flight plans, search and rescue starts 30 minutes after ETA if the plan isn't closed, and a separate flight plan is recommended for each leg of a trip with stops.`},
        {t:`Skip the flight plans and stay on flight following the whole way.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Flight following stops at Greeley. Radar service terminated, remember? And if you went down in a field on the way, the controller might notice the target's gone, or might be busy and not. A flight plan is somebody whose job is to notice."</span> Jordan files them.`,
          why:`Flight following is a radar advisory service, provided workload permitting, and ends at nontowered airports. A VFR flight plan is what triggers search and rescue if you don't arrive. They do different jobs.`},
        {t:`File one plan per leg, then open and close each one with Flight Service.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Three plans, three ETAs. She writes the closing times on her kneeboard in red, under each leg, with the half hour added: <b>CLOSE BY 10:03. CLOSE BY 11:10. CLOSE BY 12:30.</b> Then she texts Abby the times, because Abby says to tell somebody too and Abby is somebody. Abby: <b>close every one. I'll know.</b> Then: <b>BEND is a good name.</b>`,
          why:`The AIM recommends a separate VFR flight plan for each leg of a stopover flight, activated by the pilot after departure and closed by the pilot after landing. Search and rescue begins if a plan isn't closed within 30 minutes of ETA.`},
        {t:`File one plan per leg, each with an assumed departure time that opens it.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You can. The AIM even lets you. Then you're late at Greeley because the fuel truck's slow, and search and rescue's clock started at the time you guessed. Who updates it?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Me."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"From the fuel pit. Or you open it on the radio after takeoff like everybody else."</span>`,
          why:`Assumed departure times are allowed, but the AIM cautions that search and rescue runs off that time if you don't update it. Opening the plan after takeoff keeps the clock honest when stops run long.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s7`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.C`, title:`The known conditions`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday, November 22, 6:40 in the morning, the briefing room, still dark outside. Abby had come in on a Sunday to review the planning, which 61.93 said she had to, and she'd brought two breakfast burritos from the place on Arapahoe Road, which nothing said she had to. She pushed one across the table without comment.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Weather, printed at 6:32 AM"><code>METAR KAPA 221253Z 32006KT 10SM FEW120 M04/M12 A3016 RMK AO2<br><br>TAF KAPA 221120Z 2212/2312 32008KT P6SM FEW120<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM221800 34008KT P6SM SCT025<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;PROB30 2221/2224 4SM -SHSN BKN060<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FM230100 34006KT P6SM SKC<br><br>FB DATA BASED ON 220600Z, VALID 221200Z, FOR USE 0900-1800Z<br>FT&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;6000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;9000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;12000&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;18000<br>DEN&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3216-06&nbsp;3022-12&nbsp;2934-24<br><br>GFA, PLAINS E AND NE OF DENVER: ISOLD -SHSN DEVELOPING AFT 17Z. BASES 8000-9000 MSL. VIS 1-3SM IN SHSN.</code></prop>`,`She did the times out loud. Twenty one hundred Zulu, minus seven: two in the afternoon. Seventeen Zulu: ten in the morning. The TAF only covered five miles around Centennial. The graphical forecast covered the space in between, which was where she'd actually be.`,`${G.nopersonalmins?`Abby's limits were copied onto the card above the mag switch, and she knew them by heart.`:`Her card was above the mag switch. On Monday, at the kitchen table, with Abby on speaker, she'd added a line at the bottom for cross-countries: CEILING 5,000 AGL, VIS 10, AND A WAY OUT ON EVERY LEG.`} Abby finished her burrito. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Tell me the plan. Then tell me what turns you around."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Cancel the trip, and fly it on a day with no showers in the forecast.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I'll sign a cancel any day. But read it again. Isolated, after ten, bases at eight or nine thousand, and the TAF's PROB30 is after two. Isolated means you can see each one coming and keep five miles from it. You'll be home by noon."</span> She waits. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Accept no unnecessary risk. Necessary risk you plan for. What would make it a plan?"</span> Jordan looks at the GFA line for a while and then tells her: out by 8:40, home by 1:00, east around anything that builds, back to Greeley if east isn't open. Abby nods. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Now that I'll sign."</span>`,
          why:`Canceling is always safe. But the forecast showed isolated showers late in the morning, and none at the airport until after 2 PM, with a flight planned to finish by noon. Risk management also means accepting a planned risk when there's margin and a clear way out.`},
        {t:`Go on the TAF's timing, and handle any showers when she sees them.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The TAF is five miles around this building. You're not flying in this building."</span> She taps the GFA line. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Isolated, after ten, out east. That's your route at ten fifteen. What do you do when one's on your line?"</span> Jordan doesn't have an answer. She makes one, at the table, and writes it on the log: east around it, never west, or back to Greeley. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Now."</span>`,
          why:`A TAF covers the area within 5 statute miles of the airport. Between airports you need the area products like the GFA. Deciding in flight, with no plan, is how a pilot ends up choosing between bad options. 91.103 calls for alternatives before you go.`},
        {t:`Go now instead, wheels up at seven, and be home before anything builds.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"In twenty minutes. With a preflight you haven't done, a plan you'd be rereading on the runup, and fuel you haven't dipped."</span> She doesn't sound angry. That's worse. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Quiet, level, very serious.">"Nothing in that forecast happens before ten. The only thing chasing you is you. Your first one alone, you don't race anything. Racing the weather is a thing your brother did. I've heard."</span>`,
          why:`Hurrying a first solo cross-country to beat a forecast squeezes the preflight and planning that make the flight safe, and here the forecast didn't call for showers before 10 AM anyway. A planned departure, a finish time with margin, and a briefed way out manage weather risk. A race against it doesn't.`},
        {t:`Leave at 8:40 and be home by one, east around anything that builds.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She lays it out in order, the way she'd hand off a patient. Departure 8:40, Greeley by 9:35, fuel and close and file, out by 10:05, Space Port by 10:40, home by noon. One o'clock is the line. A shower on any leg: east around it, at least five miles, because west of the route is the Bravo. If east isn't open, back to Greeley and wait. Abby listens to all of it, nodding at nothing, and then uncaps the green pen and writes the second endorsement in her logbook, slowly, the way she never writes anything. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Quiet, deliberate, approving.">"Known conditions. That's the phrase in the reg. You just told me what they are."</span>`,
          why:`61.93(d) requires the instructor to review current and forecast weather and determine the flight can be completed under VFR. A planned finish time with margin, a defined turn-back point, and a pre-planned diversion are what the PHAK calls managing weather risk before the flight.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s8`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.D`, title:`Twenty four gallons`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The club chat from the night before was still open on her phone.`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="Sat 6:12 PM">biscuit's back from the burger run. 27 by the stick, 13 and a half a side, so 24 usable. self serve card reader at APA is down again, sorry, not my fault, i blame the reader</prop>`,`<prop type="chat" from="Marisol Tran" time="Sat 6:20 PM">Truck can come Sunday, there's a callout fee. Greeley has fuel. Jordan, the selector's sticking short of BOTH again. Lift and twist, feel for the click, don't force it.</prop>`,`7:35 AM. Minus four on the ramp and the sky clean and pale over the plains. Walt was at the tie down, which on a Sunday morning surprised her until she remembered he'd been a dispatcher for thirty years and probably hadn't slept in on a weekend since the eighties. He had a coffee in each hand and gave her one. She dipped both tanks with the clear tube. Thirteen and a half a side, near enough. Twenty four usable.`,`Walt looked at her nav log over his glasses. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Every release I ever signed had one number on it I cared about more than the others. What do you land with?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fly all three legs on the 24 she has, landing above the 91.151 reserve.`, s:0, flag:`thinfuel`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Legal."</span> He drinks his coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"In my old job we had a name for landing with exactly the legal number. We called it the number you don't plan to. Your plan has no room in it for anything that isn't on the paper."</span> Jordan says it'll be fine, the weather's good. Walt says <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Mm,"</span> and lets her go, and she sees him write something on the back of an envelope as she taxis.`,
          why:`91.151 is a minimum to the first landing, not a planning target. 24 gallons for an 18.1 gallon day leaves about 44 minutes at the end with nothing left for a diversion, a delay, or a wrong wind. A thin legal reserve is still thin.`},
        {t:`Fly leg one on the 24, then top off at Greeley for the rest of the day.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Leg one is 8.1. I land at Greeley with about 16, two hours. I top off there and land everywhere after that with two or more."</span> Walt nods slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"And if Greeley's pump is down too?"</span> She hadn't thought of that. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Then I have 16 at Greeley, and the rest of the day needs 10 plus my hour. I'd wait or call the truck. I wouldn't leave."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"That's an alternate,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Most students think an alternate is a place. It's a decision you made before you needed it."</span>`,
          why:`Planning fuel at every stop, with a reserve well above the 91.151 minimum and a backup if fuel isn't available, follows the PHAK's guidance to add an adequate reserve and the ACS's fuel planning risk element.`},
        {t:`Leave on the 24, and decide about fuel at Greeley from the gauges.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"The gauges."</span> He looks at Biscuit the way you'd look at a dog that's eaten a shoe. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Those gauges are required to read empty accurately when they're empty. That's about all they promise. Dip it at Greeley and decide now what number makes you buy."</span> She writes <b>BUY AT KGXY</b> on the log.`,
          why:`Deferring the fuel decision to the gauges relies on instruments that are often inaccurate in older airplanes. Decide the fuel plan before departure and verify quantity by dipping the tanks.`},
        {t:`Plan all three legs on the 27 she and Russ both got by the stick.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Walt taps the 27 with one finger. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"That's what's in the tanks. Three of it never gets to the engine. Plan on what you can burn."</span> She crosses it out, writes 24, and works the day again from there.`,
          why:`Fuel planning counts usable fuel only. The unusable fuel stays in the tanks and lines, so 27 by the stick is 24 to plan with, and the reserve has to come out of that.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s9`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`VI`, title:`Ready for departure`, q:`Her endorsement requires flight following on every leg. When and how should Jordan ask for it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`8:12 AM. A text from her mother.`,`<prop type="text" from="Lourdes Reyes" time="8:12 AM">Where today.</prop>`,`<prop type="text" from="Jordan Reyes" time="8:13 AM">Greeley, then a stop near Watkins, then home. About three hours.</prop>`,`<prop type="text" from="Lourdes Reyes" time="8:15 AM">Text me when you're down. Each time.</prop>`,`In the flight bag, next to the green notebook, her logbook lay open to the new page.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, endorsements">11/19/26. I certify that Jordan E. Reyes has received the required solo cross-country training. I find they have met the applicable requirements of 14 CFR § 61.93 and are proficient to make solo cross-country flights in a Cessna 172N aircraft, airplane single-engine.<br>Limitations: Day VFR only. Ceiling 5,000 AGL or higher and visibility 10 SM or better at departure, destination, and en route reporting points. Remain at least 5 SM from any precipitation. Surface wind 10 knots or less, gusts no more than 5 over, crosswind component 5 knots or less. FLIGHT FOLLOWING ON EVERY LEG. No Class B. On the ground at KAPA by 1300 local.<br>/s/ 11/19/26 A. Strand 3418862CFI RE 06-30-2027<br><br>11/22/26. I have reviewed the cross-country planning of Jordan E. Reyes. I find the planning and preparation to be correct to make the solo flight from KAPA to KAPA via BEND, Strasburg, and Roggen with landings at KGXY and KCFO in a Cessna 172N aircraft on 11/22/26. Limitations of the 11/19/26 endorsement apply and are met.<br>/s/ 11/22/26 A. Strand 3418862CFI RE 06-30-2027</prop>`,`${G.noflightfollowing?`The capitals were for her. In July, on the trip to Rocky Mountain Metro, she'd skipped flight following, and a Bonanza had gone by close enough that Abby had said what it was without looking it up.`:`The capitals weren't for her, Abby had said, they were for the endorsement. Jordan had decided to believe that.`}`,`Runup on Alpha near the end of three five right, the left door latched top and bottom, both of them touched. Mags, carb heat, the selector lifted and twisted to BOTH until it clicked. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Centennial Ground, Niner One Echo, ready for departure, runway three five right."</span> <span class="said" data-who="man">"Niner One Echo, monitor tower."</span> Then tower, and the takeoff, wind three two zero at six, right turn eastbound approved, the door holding, the reservoir coming up blue and flat on the left of the nose, and then the tower: <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Niner One Echo, frequency change approved."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask tower to set up flight following, and stay with tower until the handoff.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She asks. The tower controller says, kindly, that she'd need to contact Approach herself, and Jordan says <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Contacting Approach, Niner One Echo,"</span> and does. Ninety seconds she spent on the wrong frequency, climbing, under a shelf.`,
          why:`Some towers will coordinate flight following on departure, but often the pilot contacts Approach directly after leaving the Class D. Ask early, or plan to call Approach yourself once the tower releases you.`},
        {t:`Wait until she's past the reservoir where it's quieter, then decide about calling.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She flies on in silence for a minute and then remembers the capitals in her logbook. She calls Approach. Over Aurora, a Cherokee crosses below her that she hadn't seen. The controller had been about to tell her.`,
          why:`Her endorsement required flight following on every leg, and 61.89(a)(8) says a student can't fly contrary to a limitation her instructor put in her logbook. Beyond that, the busiest airspace on the route is the departure area under the shelves.`},
        {t:`Call Denver Approach once tower lets her go, and request flight following to Greeley.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Denver Approach, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, four miles east of Centennial, climbing through seven thousand for seven thousand five hundred, request flight following to Greeley."</span> A man's voice gives her a code. She reads it back and dials it in, and he says <span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, radar contact, four miles east of Centennial, maintain VFR at or below seven thousand five hundred."</span> Level at 7,500 she asks him for a minute off the frequency, opens the flight plan with Flight Service, and comes back, and BEND comes up on the GPS where she put it at the kitchen table, and the reservoir slides under the nose right on time.`,
          why:`AIM 4-1-15: radar traffic advisories are available to VFR pilots on request. The request gives who, where, altitude, and destination. Opening the VFR flight plan after takeoff starts the search and rescue clock at the real departure time.`},
        {t:`Get the squawk code, then turn the volume down to concentrate on flying.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She turns it down. Four minutes later she turns it back up and hears <span class="said" data-who="man">"Niner One Echo, Denver, how do you hear?"</span> for what is clearly not the first time. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Loud and clear, Niner One Echo, sorry."</span> <span class="said" data-who="man">"Traffic's no factor now. Keep the volume where I can reach you."</span>`,
          why:`The AIM says pilots receiving VFR radar advisories should monitor the frequency at all times. A controller who can't reach you can't tell you about traffic and has to worry that you've lost your radio or worse.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s10`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`VI`, title:`A mile left`, q:`Past Strasburg, Jordan's a mile left of course, and the heading indicator reads four off the compass. What now?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`8:57, Strasburg, right on time: the interstate, the grain elevator, the little grid of streets, and she turned north onto 347 and wrote the time on the log. Three miles later she stepped down to 6,700 for the 70.`,`9:04. By the log she should have been ten miles past Strasburg at 9:03 and a half. She checked her position against a section road crossing she'd marked, and then against the GPS, which agreed with the road: ten miles north of Strasburg, a mile left of the line. West. Toward Denver. Off to her left, fifteen miles or so, the white peaks of Denver International's tent roof sat on the horizon like laundry somebody forgot to bring in.`,`Heading indicator first, then the compass. Gyro, 347. The compass wobbled around for a bit and then settled on 343.`,`Sixteen miles to Roggen.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Center the GPS needle and fly whatever heading keeps it there.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She chases the magenta needle for a while. Works, more or less. She gets to Roggen and has no idea why she drifted, which bugs her the whole way to Greeley. That afternoon, at the briefing room table, Abby asks what the wind was. Jordan doesn't know. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"So the box quits. Some day it will. You're a mile off and getting farther and you don't know which way to turn."</span> Abby taps the nav log with the back of her pen. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The log's how you know."</span>`,
          why:`Following GPS guidance gets you back on course, but without comparing planned and actual results you never learn what the wind and the airplane are doing. The ACS asks for planned versus actual results and corrections.`},
        {t:`Hold her heading until I-76 and Roggen show up, and sort it out then.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She holds it. A mile becomes two, then two and a bit. Approach comes on: <span class="said" data-who="man" data-tone="Firm, urgent, clipped.">"Niner One Echo, you're drifting west, the Bravo goes to the surface about four miles west of you, say intentions."</span> She turns right, hard, and the needle comes back. Her face stays hot all the way to Roggen. Sixteen miles of it.`,
          why:`A known drift error grows with every mile, and here it drifted toward lower Class B floors. Use the 60 to 1 rule to correct as soon as you see it.`},
        {t:`Turn six degrees right to stop the drift, and fly the rest parallel to the line.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The drift stops. A mile left of the line, she reaches the interstate a mile west of Roggen and has to look for it. Abby, later: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Six to stop it, and something more to get back. Otherwise you're parallel to your plan and near your shelf all the way."</span>`,
          why:`A 6 degree correction stops the drift but leaves the airplane parallel to course. To return to the planned line by the next checkpoint, add the angle needed to close the error: 1 mile in 16 is about 4 degrees.`},
        {t:`Reset the gyro, then correct back onto the line by Roggen.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Gyro first: wings level, speed steady, the middle of the compass swing, 343. She resets it. So she'd been flying 343 the whole time, four degrees left of the plan, and that's most of the mile. The rest is wind. One mile off in ten is six degrees of track error: four from the gyro, two from the wind. So the heading that holds the line is 349. One mile back in sixteen is about four more. She turns to 353 on the newly honest gyro and watches the GPS needle start to walk back toward center. Ten miles in seven minutes is 86 knots, not 92: more headwind than the forecast. Sixteen miles at 86 is 11.3 minutes. <b>ROGGEN 0915.</b> At 9:15 the needle touches center and the interstate and the railroad come out from under the nose together, and she says <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Roggen"</span> to nobody.`,
          why:`The heading indicator drifts and must be reset to the compass. The 60 to 1 rule turns the mile into degrees: 6 degrees of track error to take out, plus about 4 to get back on the line by the next checkpoint. A groundspeed from actual time and distance gives the corrected ETA.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s11`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`VI`, title:`The curtain`, q:`A snow shower is drifting southeast across Jordan's return line. How should she divert?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Greeley went by the way it was supposed to. A left downwind for 35 behind nobody, a landing she was proud of, ${(s.flags.thinfuel||G.thinfuel)?`a walk around Biscuit on the ramp`:`the fuel pit`}, the flight plan closed and the next one filed, a text to her mother: <b>Down at Greeley.</b> Her mother: <b>Ok.</b> She was off again at 10:05 with Approach giving her a new code, southeast at 7,500 toward Roggen, the low sun just right of the nose.`,`She saw it from fifteen miles out and didn't understand it at first. Ahead, a little right of the nose, a piece of the sky had gone gray. A flat dark base, maybe eight thousand feet, and under it a soft curtain reaching all the way to the fields, with lighter streamers hanging off its edges that didn't reach anything. It hadn't been there at Greeley. By Roggen, 10:17, it was right on her line to Strasburg, about twelve miles ahead, a few miles across, drifting southeast the way the winds aloft said it would. Slow.`,`To its right, west, the sky was clear all the way to the tent roof and the shelves. To its left, east, clear and bright over empty country all the way to I-70. Behind her Greeley sat in sunshine.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Go around the west side of it, the shorter way to the Space Port from Roggen.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`She banks west. In a minute Approach is on her: <span class="said" data-who="man">"Niner One Echo, the Bravo floor ahead of you is seven thousand, and the surface area is about five miles beyond that. Say intentions."</span> She tells him she'll turn east around the weather. It costs twenty minutes, a long loop behind the shower as it drifts south, and she lands at the Space Port close to eleven with her shoulders up around her ears.`,
          why:`West of the route lay lower Class B floors and the surface area of the Denver Class B. The shorter way around isn't shorter if it ends in airspace she can't enter. Her plan said east for exactly this reason.`},
        {t:`Turn back to Greeley, land, and wait for it to blow through.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`She turns north and tells Approach. Greeley's still in sunshine. Ten minutes later, from the downwind at Greeley, she can see the gray drifting south off her line, and the air behind it clear and dry. She lands anyway, because that was the plan, waits twenty minutes in the FBO, and flies the leg again behind it. She lands at the Space Port close to eleven. Abby, later: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Nothing wrong with that. You had a better one written down, though, and you'd briefed it."</span>`,
          why:`Returning to a known good airport in clear weather is always a safe diversion. Here the plan she'd briefed, a wide deviation to the clear east side, was also safe and was the one she'd prepared for. Either works; the briefed plan cost less.`},
        {t:`Drop to 6,500 and slide under the lighter edge of it, where she can see through.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`She starts down. At three miles from the edge the fields under it disappear, not slowly, all at once, like a door shutting. She turns back east the way Abby taught her under the hood, wings level, standard rate, eyes on the attitude indicator, and comes out into sunlight with her hands wet inside her gloves. She goes around the east side. She lands at the Space Port close to eleven and sits in the airplane a long time before she gets out.`,
          why:`Visibility in snow showers can drop to near zero instantly, and a VFR pilot in it has lost the horizon. Going under or into precipitation you can't see through is how VFR pilots end up in instrument conditions. Give showers miles.`},
        {t:`Hold her heading past Roggen, well east of it, then turn for Strasburg.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`She doesn't turn at Roggen. She notes the time, 10:17, and keeps the heading she has, 138, which runs southeast down the clear side. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Denver Approach, Niner One Echo, deviating east for a snow shower, then direct Strasburg."</span> <span class="said" data-who="man">"Niner One Echo, roger, no traffic observed in that direction."</span> On the kneeboard: 19 miles to a point ten miles east of her line, out ahead of the shower because it's drifting toward her return leg, 121 knots, nine and a half minutes. Then a true course of 223 to Strasburg, 14.8 miles, which the crab and the variation bring back to 223 on the compass, 106 knots, eight and a half minutes. <b>STRASBURG 1035.</b> Four and a half minutes longer than the plan, and six tenths of a gallon. At 10:26 she turns. The shower slides by on her right, close enough to see the snow inside it moving, the streamers of virga glowing white where the sun gets under them, and then it's behind her and I-70 is ahead, and Strasburg is exactly where she left it.`,
          why:`The PHAK describes a pre-planned diversion around hazardous weather: start at a recognizable point, note the time, compute heading, groundspeed, ETA, and fuel, and fly the airplane. East had clear air and higher Class B floors. The drift was toward her return leg, which is why the turn point went wide enough to keep her about five miles from the edge.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s12`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.B`, title:`Caution wake turbulence`, q:`Landing behind a jet at the Space Port, where should Jordan's approach and touchdown be?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Strasburg, then west down the interstate toward the Space Port with the ATIS in her ear: wind three three zero at eight, visibility one zero, few clouds at two thousand five hundred, landing runway three five. She was under the 80. She called the tower from eight miles east, then stepped down to 6,500 as she passed under the 70.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Niner One Echo, Space Port Tower, enter right downwind runway three five, report midfield."</span>`,`On the downwind, with the long runway off her right wing, the tower came back. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Niner One Echo, extend downwind, number two following a Citation on a four mile final. Caution wake turbulence."</span> She found it, a small white jet low on final, gear down, catching the sun. She called it in sight. It looked harmless from there. Abby had told her what they all look like from there.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Stay at or above the jet's glidepath, and land beyond where it touched down.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`The tower calls her base. She turns final a little high on purpose and keeps the picture steep, above where the jet came down, and watches for its touchdown: a puff off the mains about a thousand feet down the runway. She aims for a spot well past it, which on 8,000 feet of runway costs her nothing at all. Her wheels touch beyond the puff. The Citation is already turning off ahead of her, small and white and harmless, and she lets out a breath she'd been keeping since the downwind.`,
          why:`AIM 7-4-6: when landing behind a larger aircraft on the same runway, stay at or above its final approach flight path, note its touchdown point, and land beyond it. Wake vortices sink and trail behind and below the aircraft that made them.`},
        {t:`Fly a lower, flatter approach under its path and touch down short of it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`She starts the approach low. On a mile final the airplane rolls right, hard, without asking her, twenty degrees before she catches it with full left aileron. She adds power and goes around. Tower: <span class="said" data-who="man">"Niner One Echo, go around approved, I'll call your crosswind."</span> On the second approach she stays high and lands long.`,
          why:`Wake vortices sink below the flight path of the aircraft that generated them. Flying under its path puts a light airplane in the vortices, which can roll it beyond its control authority close to the ground.`},
        {t:`Extend the downwind for extra spacing, then land on the numbers as usual.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She extends. The extra time helps, the wake drifting and decaying, and the landing is fine. Abby, later, reading the times on her log: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Time helps. Geometry helps more. Above its path, past its touchdown. Then you don't need to hope the time was enough."</span>`,
          why:`Extra spacing lets wake turbulence decay and drift, which helps. But landing short of the larger aircraft's touchdown point can still put you in its vortices. The AIM procedure is to stay above its path and land beyond its touchdown point.`},
        {t:`Turn base right behind it and keep the spacing tight for the tower.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="man" data-tone="Firm, clipped, a little annoyed.">"Niner One Echo, I said extend. Continue downwind, I'll call your base."</span> She extends, embarrassed, and lands well past the Citation's touchdown point, high and steep, because the voice on the radio had made her think about it.`,
          why:`The tower told her to extend, so turning early would be not following an ATC instruction. And tight spacing behind a jet is exactly when wake turbulence is most dangerous to a light airplane.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f9s2`, title:`One row of the nav log`,
     setup:[`The leg from Strasburg to Roggen. True course 358 off the plotter, 26.2 nautical miles. True airspeed 105 knots. Wind from the FB at 9,000: 320 true at 16 knots. Variation 7 E. Burn 8.0 gallons per hour.`,`Biscuit's compass card: FOR N STEER 001, FOR 330 STEER 330. Use the nearest card heading.`,`Wind angle is wind direction minus true course. WCA = asin(wind speed × sin(wind angle) ÷ TAS), positive is right. GS = TAS × cos(WCA) minus wind speed × cos(wind angle). Round the WCA to the nearest degree before building the heading. Enter clock times as numbers without the colon, so 9:14 is 914.`],
     fields:[{label:`Wind correction angle (degrees, left is negative)`,answer:-5,tol:0},{label:`True heading`,answer:353,tol:0},{label:`Magnetic heading`,answer:346,tol:0},{label:`Compass heading`,answer:347,tol:0},{label:`Groundspeed (knots)`,answer:92,tol:1},{label:`Time en route (minutes)`,answer:17.1,tol:0.2},{label:`Fuel for the leg (gallons)`,answer:2.3,tol:0.1},{label:`ETA at Roggen if over Strasburg at 8:57`,answer:914,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Wind angle: 320 minus 358 = minus 38 degrees. The wind is from 38 degrees left of the nose.`,`sin(minus 38°) = minus 0.6157. 16 × minus 0.6157 = minus 9.851. Divide by 105: minus 0.0938. asin(minus 0.0938) = minus 5.38°, so 5 degrees left.`,`True heading: 358 minus 5 = 353. Magnetic: 353 minus 7 (east is least) = 346. Nearest card heading is N, which says steer 001, one more: compass heading 347.`,`Groundspeed: 105 × cos(5.38°) = 105 × 0.9956 = 104.54. 16 × cos(minus 38°) = 16 × 0.7880 = 12.61. 104.54 minus 12.61 = 91.93, about 92 knots. Check: the headwind component is 16 × cos 38 = 12.6 knots, and 105 minus 12.6, less a hair for the crab, is 92.`,`Time: 26.2 ÷ 91.93 = 0.285 hours × 60 = 17.1 minutes. Fuel: 17.1 ÷ 60 × 8.0 = 2.28, about 2.3 gallons.`,`ETA: 8:57 plus 17.1 minutes is 9:14.1, so 9:14.`]},
    {after:`f9s8`, title:`Fuel, the dispatcher's way`,
     setup:[`From the nav log: leg 1 cruise fuel 6.1 gallons, leg 2 cruise 3.9, leg 3 cruise 2.1. Each departure adds 1.0 gallon for start, taxi, and runup and 1.0 gallon for the climb. Burn 8.0 gallons per hour at cruise. On board at Centennial: 24 gallons usable (27 by the dipstick, less 3 unusable).`,`91.151(a)(1): enough fuel to the first point of intended landing plus 30 minutes at normal cruise, by day. The club's rule and Jordan's: land with an hour.`],
     fields:[{label:`Leg 1 fuel, including taxi and climb (gallons)`,answer:8.1,tol:0.05},{label:`91.151 day reserve at 8.0 gal/hr (gallons)`,answer:4,tol:0},{label:`Legal minimum on board to start leg 1 (gallons)`,answer:12.1,tol:0.05},{label:`Fuel for the whole day (gallons)`,answer:18.1,tol:0.05},{label:`Left at home if she buys none (gallons)`,answer:5.9,tol:0.05},{label:`That reserve in minutes`,answer:44,tol:1},{label:`Fuel needed at KCFO to legally start leg 3 (gallons)`,answer:8.1,tol:0.05},{label:`Minimum to buy at Greeley to land at Centennial with an hour (gallons)`,answer:2.1,tol:0.05}],
     solution:[`Leg 1: 6.1 + 1.0 + 1.0 = 8.1 gallons. Leg 2: 3.9 + 2.0 = 5.9. Leg 3: 2.1 + 2.0 = 4.1. Day: 8.1 + 5.9 + 4.1 = 18.1.`,`Reserve: 30 minutes at 8.0 gal/hr is 0.5 × 8.0 = 4.0 gallons. Legal to start leg 1: 8.1 + 4.0 = 12.1. She has 24. Legal by almost double.`,`With no fuel bought: 24 minus 18.1 = 5.9 gallons at the end. 5.9 ÷ 8.0 = 0.74 hours × 60 = 44 minutes. Legal at every departure. Under the hour.`,`Leg 3 needs 4.1 + 4.0 = 8.1 on board at the Space Port. If the day goes exactly to plan, she'd arrive there with 24 minus 8.1 minus 5.9 = 10.0, legal by 1.9 gallons. Any detour longer than about 14 minutes at 8 gallons an hour eats that.`,`To land home with an hour: she arrives at Greeley with 24 minus 8.1 = 15.9. The rest of the day is 5.9 + 4.1 = 10.0, plus 8.0 for the hour, so she needs 18.0. 18.0 minus 15.9 = 2.1 gallons, minimum. She'll top off, because the pump is right there and the minimum is a number you don't plan to.`]},
    {after:`f9s11`, title:`Around it`,
     setup:[`Over Roggen at 10:17. The shower sits 12 miles down her planned line to Strasburg, drifting southeast toward her return leg. She'll hold the course she came in on, past Roggen to a turn point about ten miles east of her line, then turn for Strasburg.`,`From the chart: Roggen to the turn point, true course 144, 19.0 nautical miles. Turn point to Strasburg, true course 223, 14.8 nautical miles. The planned leg straight from Roggen to Strasburg was 26.2 miles at 117 knots, 13.4 minutes.`,`TAS 105, wind 320 true at 16 knots, variation 7 E, burn 8.0 gallons per hour. Compass card: FOR 150 STEER 150, FOR 210 STEER 208, FOR 240 STEER 238. Round the WCA to the nearest degree. Clock times without the colon.`],
     fields:[{label:`Groundspeed, Roggen to the turn point (knots)`,answer:121,tol:1},{label:`Minutes to the turn point`,answer:9.4,tol:0.2},{label:`True heading, turn point to Strasburg`,answer:232,tol:0},{label:`Magnetic heading for that leg`,answer:225,tol:0},{label:`Compass heading for that leg`,answer:223,tol:0},{label:`Groundspeed, turn point to Strasburg (knots)`,answer:106,tol:1},{label:`Minutes, turn point to Strasburg`,answer:8.4,tol:0.2},{label:`Extra minutes compared with the plan`,answer:4.4,tol:0.2},{label:`Extra fuel (gallons)`,answer:0.59,tol:0.05},{label:`New ETA at Strasburg`,answer:1035,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Leg A, course 144. Wind angle 320 minus 144 = 176°. sin 176° = 0.0698. 16 × 0.0698 ÷ 105 = 0.0106, asin = 0.61°, about 1 right: true heading 145, magnetic 138, and the card near 150 says no correction, so compass 138, the same heading she came in on from Greeley. GS = 105 × cos(0.61°) minus 16 × cos 176° = 104.99 minus (minus 15.96) = 121.0 knots, nearly a pure tailwind. Time: 19.0 ÷ 121.0 × 60 = 9.4 minutes.`,`Leg B, course 223. Wind angle 320 minus 223 = 97°. sin 97° = 0.9925. 16 × 0.9925 ÷ 105 = 0.1512, asin = 8.70°, so 9 right. True heading 223 + 9 = 232. Magnetic 232 minus 7 = 225. That's halfway between the 210 and 240 card headings, and both say steer two less: compass 223.`,`GS = 105 × cos(8.70°) minus 16 × cos 97° = 103.79 minus (minus 1.95) = 105.7 knots. Time: 14.8 ÷ 105.7 × 60 = 8.4 minutes.`,`Total: 9.4 + 8.4 = 17.8 minutes, against 13.4 planned. Extra: 4.4 minutes. Fuel: 4.4 ÷ 60 × 8.0 = 0.59 gallons. Distance: 19.0 + 14.8 = 33.8 against 26.2, 7.6 miles more.`,`ETA: 10:17 + 17.8 = 10:34.8, so 10:35. Four and a half minutes and six tenths of a gallon is the price of not flying into a snow shower. That's the cheapest thing she bought all day.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f9s4`, title:`Needles and north`, intro:`On the drive home from the dual, at red lights, Jordan quizzed herself out loud from a list Abby had written on the back of the sticky note pad. Pick one for each.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Variation 7° E. True heading 353°. Magnetic heading?`, choices:[`346°`,`360°`,`353°`], answer:0, why:`East is least: subtract east variation. 353 minus 7 = 346.`},
      {prompt:`The card says FOR 180 STEER 178. Magnetic heading 176°. Compass heading?`, choices:[`178°`,`174°`,`176°`], answer:1, why:`The card shows minus 2 near south. 176 minus 2 = 174.`},
      {prompt:`Turning to a heading of north by the magnetic compass, in Colorado, you should roll out`, choices:[`after the compass passes north`,`before the compass reaches north`,`exactly as it reads north`], answer:1, why:`In the northern hemisphere the compass lags when turning to north. Undershoot north. The PHAK's rule of thumb is about 15 degrees plus half the latitude early.`},
      {prompt:`Accelerating on an easterly heading, the magnetic compass shows`, choices:[`a turn toward north`,`a turn toward south`,`no change at all`], answer:0, why:`ANDS: Accelerate North, Decelerate South, on easterly or westerly headings.`},
      {prompt:`OBS on 030, needle centered, TO flag. You are on`, choices:[`the 030 radial`,`the 210 radial`,`the 120 radial`], answer:1, why:`With a TO flag, flying 030 takes you to the station, so you're southwest of it, on the 210 radial. A FROM flag would put you on the 030 radial.`},
      {prompt:`The AIM's only positive way to identify a VOR`, choices:[`its Morse code or recorded voice ID`,`a steady, centered needle`,`a controller naming the station`], answer:0, why:`AIM 1-1-3: only the Morse code identifier or the recorded voice identification positively identifies a VOR.`},
      {prompt:`Maximum error for an airborne VOR check, by the IFR standard`, choices:[`plus or minus 4 degrees`,`plus or minus 6 degrees`,`plus or minus 10 degrees`], answer:1, why:`AIM 1-1-4: plus or minus 4 for a ground check, plus or minus 6 for an airborne check.`},
      {prompt:`Two VOR receivers tuned to the same station may differ by no more than`, choices:[`4 degrees`,`6 degrees`,`2 degrees`], answer:0, why:`The dual receiver check allows a maximum difference of 4 degrees between the two indicated bearings.`},
      {prompt:`The bearing to a VOR off your wingtip changes 10° in 2 minutes. Minutes to the station?`, choices:[`5`,`12`,`20`], answer:1, why:`Seconds ÷ degrees = minutes. 120 ÷ 10 = 12 minutes.`},
      {prompt:`One degree off a VOR radial, 60 miles from the station, is about`, choices:[`6 miles`,`1 mile`,`a tenth of a mile`], answer:1, why:`The 60 to 1 relationship: a degree is about a mile wide at 60 NM and half a mile at 30 NM.`},
      {prompt:`On a sectional chart, one inch is about`, choices:[`6.86 nautical miles`,`10 nautical miles`,`1 nautical mile`], answer:0, why:`Sectional scale is 1:500,000. One inch is about 8 statute miles, which is 6.86 nautical.`},
      {prompt:`Unsure where you are, the PHAK's advice about your heading`, choices:[`Hold it`,`Turn around`,`Circle until sure`], answer:0, why:`"If confused, hold the heading." Turning away from it is an easy way to get more lost.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`True course 079°. Wind correction angle 8° left. Variation 7° E. The magnetic heading is`, opts:[`078°`,`064°`,`080°`], a:1, why:`TC 079 minus 8 = TH 071. East variation is subtracted: 071 minus 7 = MH 064. Adding the variation gives 078, the common error.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`Under 14 CFR 91.151, a VFR flight in an airplane by day must carry enough fuel to the first point of intended landing and then fly at normal cruise for at least`, opts:[`30 minutes`,`45 minutes`,`20 minutes`], a:0, why:`91.151(a)(1): 30 minutes by day. 45 minutes is the night requirement. 20 minutes is the rotorcraft rule.`, task:`I.D`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`VFR on a magnetic course of 165°, cruising about 4,000 feet above terrain that is at 3,500 feet MSL, an appropriate altitude is`, opts:[`8,500 feet MSL`,`8,000 feet MSL`,`7,500 feet MSL`], a:2, why:`91.159: magnetic course 0 through 179, odd thousands plus 500. 7,500 MSL is 4,000 AGL here, above the 3,000 foot threshold, so the rule applies. 8,500 is for westbound.`, task:`I.D`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`The VFR cruising altitude rule of 91.159 applies to level cruising flight more than`, opts:[`3,000 feet above the surface`,`3,000 feet above sea level`,`1,500 feet above the surface`], a:0, why:`91.159: in level cruising flight more than 3,000 feet above the surface. Under low Class B shelves, pilots are often below that and the rule doesn't apply.`, task:`I.D`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`According to the AIM, a pilot can positively identify a VOR by`, opts:[`a centered needle with a TO flag`,`its Morse code or recorded voice ID`,`a controller confirming its name`], a:1, why:`AIM 1-1-3: the only positive method is the Morse code identification or the recorded voice identification. Don't rely on a facility's voice transmissions.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`A VOR radial is`, opts:[`a magnetic bearing to the station`,`a true bearing from the station`,`a magnetic bearing from the station`], a:2, why:`Radials are magnetic courses outbound from the station. To fly to the station on the 210 radial, you fly 030.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`With a VOR off the wingtip, the bearing changes 10° in 90 seconds. The time to the station is about`, opts:[`9 minutes`,`15 minutes`,`6 minutes`], a:0, why:`Time to station in minutes = seconds ÷ degrees of bearing change. 90 ÷ 10 = 9 minutes.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`A VFR-only panel GPS without RAIM alerting that loses satellite integrity`, opts:[`will display a warning flag`,`may give no warning at all`,`can't be used for VFR at all`], a:1, why:`AIM: VFR panel mount and handheld receivers have no RAIM alerting, so a bad position may not be flagged. Cross-check with pilotage, dead reckoning, and other navigation.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`Radar traffic advisories given to a VFR pilot on flight following`, opts:[`relieve the pilot of see and avoid`,`include vectors away from traffic`,`are provided workload permitting`], a:2, why:`AIM 4-1-15: the service depends on radar limits, traffic, and workload, doesn't relieve the pilot of see and avoid, and doesn't include vectors unless the pilot requests them.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`If a VFR flight plan is not closed or updated, search and rescue procedures begin when the aircraft is overdue past its ETA by`, opts:[`30 minutes`,`60 minutes`,`2 hours`], a:0, why:`AIM 5-1-14: if you fail to report or cancel your flight plan within 1/2 hour after your ETA, search and rescue procedures are started.`, task:`I.D`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Before each solo cross-country flight, a student pilot must have, for that specific flight,`, opts:[`a fresh medical exam`,`an instructor endorsement`,`a filed VFR flight plan`], a:1, why:`61.93(c)(3): for each cross-country flight, the instructor who reviews the planning must endorse the logbook. A flight plan is recommended but not required.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`According to the PHAK, a pilot who is lost and can't see a town should first`, opts:[`descend low enough to read road signs`,`turn around and retrace the route`,`climb, mindful of traffic and weather`], a:2, why:`PHAK lost procedures: the first thing to do is climb, being mindful of traffic and weather. Altitude improves visibility, radio and navigation reception, and radar coverage.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`Spaced review, weather. A winds aloft forecast for 9,000 feet reads 3216-06. This means wind from`, opts:[`320° true at 16 knots, minus 6°C`,`320° magnetic at 16 knots, minus 6°C`,`032° true at 16 knots, minus 6°C`], a:0, why:`FB winds are in degrees true and knots, with temperature in Celsius. 3216-06 is 320° true at 16 knots, minus 6°C.`, task:`I.C`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, airport operations. On the ground during daylight with no radio, a pilot acknowledges a light gun signal by`, opts:[`rocking the wings`,`moving the ailerons or rudder`,`flashing the landing light`], a:1, why:`AIM 4-3-13: on the ground by day, move the ailerons or rudder. In flight by day, rock the wings. At night, flash the landing or navigation lights.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, emergencies. VFR with a two-way radio failure, a pilot should set the transponder to`, opts:[`7700`,`7500`,`7600`], a:2, why:`7600 is lost communications. 7700 is a general emergency and 7500 is unlawful interference.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    ((s.picks||{})[10]===3
      ?`She was on the ground at the Space Port at 10:43, which was three minutes later than the log, and she taxied to the FBO behind a lineman with orange wands who waved her in like she was a 737.`
      :`She was on the ground at the Space Port close to eleven, later than any time on her log, and she taxied to the FBO behind a lineman with orange wands who waved her in like she was a 737.`),
    `Mixture, mags, master. The propeller stopped. She sat with her hands in her lap the way she had after the discovery flight in April, and then she did the things in order. She called Flight Service and closed the plan. She texted her mother: <b>Down at the Space Port.</b> Her mother: <b>What is a space port.</b> <b>Watkins.</b> <b>Text me from home.</b>`,
    ((s.flags.thinfuel||G.thinfuel)
      ?((s.picks||{})[10]===3
        ?`Then she dipped the tanks, because Walt's envelope had been bothering her since Greeley. A little over twelve by the stick, a little over nine usable. Leg 3 needed 8.1 to be legal. She did it twice on the kneeboard and it came out the same both times: legal by about a gallon and a quarter, and the shower had already taken more than half a gallon of that. She bought ten at the self-serve pump with her own card and stood in the cold watching the numbers go up, feeling like she'd been caught at something, though there was nobody there to catch her.`
        :`Then she dipped the tanks, because Walt's envelope had been bothering her since Greeley. About ten by the stick, under eight usable. Leg 3 needed 8.1 under 91.151 before she could legally leave. The detour had eaten the margin she hadn't left herself. She stood at the self-serve pump in the cold and bought fifteen gallons with her own card, and her ears were hot the whole time, and nobody was there to see it but her.`)
      :`Then she dipped the tanks out of habit and wrote the number on the log next to the time, in pencil, the way Walt wrote everything.`),
    `Inside, the FBO was warm and empty, a television on mute with the radar loop running and a vending machine that sold hot chocolate for a dollar fifty. She got one and sat in a leather chair by the window with Danny's headset on the seat next to her. On the screen, the little green blob that had been her shower drifted southeast past Byers and came apart. Home was clear. She had two hours before Abby's line and needed fifteen minutes of them. She stayed in the chair anyway.`,
    `Somewhere in the second half of the hot chocolate she noticed it.`,
    `Since 7:10 that morning, since Abby capped the green pen, she hadn't thought about him. Not at the runup. Not at BEND, or Strasburg, or the gyro that had drifted four degrees, or Greeley, where she'd stood on the ramp with his headset around her neck and the wind going through her coat. Not at the curtain. Three and a half hours. She'd had his headset on her head the entire time, and his E6B in the side pocket with his pencil dot on it, and she'd flown his airplane to Greeley, which was a place he'd flown, and she had not thought about Danny once.`,
    `It landed on her like forgetting a birthday. Like walking past the house of somebody whose funeral you went to and noticing you'd been humming. Eight months, and the first three free hours she'd had, she'd spent them without him and hadn't noticed until they were over. She put the cup down. She looked at the headset. She felt, for a minute, about as bad as she'd felt in March.`,
    `Then she put the headset on and went home.`,
    `The leg home was fifteen minutes and nothing happened in it. South from the Space Port under the 70, BEND coming up on the GPS where she'd built it, a right turn at BEND, the reservoir sliding by on the right, Centennial Tower giving her a right downwind for three five right and a landing she made without hurrying. She closed the third flight plan from the tie down at 11:58, chained Biscuit down tight, and texted her mother <b>Home.</b> Her mother sent back a heart, which she had never done before, and then, a minute later, as if to correct the record: <b>Ok.</b>`,
    `Claire texted while she was putting the cover on.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Claire Reyes" time="12:09 PM">El wants to know if Greeley smells. She says her dad told her it did.</prop>`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Jordan Reyes" time="12:11 PM">Didn't notice. Had the vents closed. Tell her I'll ask.</prop>`,
    `She sat in the left seat with the door open and took out the green notebook. On Monday she'd found the page and folded the corner down without reading it, because she'd decided she'd read his first cross-country after she'd flown hers, and not before. It was July. Eleven years ago.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, a page from July, eleven years ago">July 18. FIRST SOLO XC!!! C152 52B from the school. KAPA, Greeley, Front Range, KAPA. 2.6 on the Hobbs. Planned 2.2. You can see where this is going.<br>Went around the east side like a good boy. Strasburg, fine. Then I got to the next interstate and followed it, and it turns out you're supposed to cross that one, because it goes to Nebraska. Climbed. Read a water tower: WIGGINS. Wiggins is not Greeley. Turned to the heading I should have been on in the first place, found the river, found Greeley by the smell. Feedlots. You could land on that smell.<br>Front Range tower lady said "Welcome to Front Range" like she meant it. Bought a Coke. Called nobody.<br>Here's the thing I want on the record. For two and a half hours I did not think about one other thing. Not work, not money, not the guy at the shop, not anything. Just the next checkpoint and the gas and the next checkpoint. Nobody tells you that's the best part. It's not the view. It's the quiet in your head.<br>Jordan thinks too much. She'd like this. She'd never admit it.</prop>`,
    `She read the last line again. Then the one before it.`,
    `The guilt from the FBO came back for a second when she got to the end, and then it went, the way a pager tone hangs in your ear after the pager's quiet. Two and a half hours of quiet in your head, he'd called it. He wrote that in July with a Coke from the Front Range FBO sweating on the page, you could see the ring, and he put her name under it. She'd gotten three and a half. He'd have been smug about that on her behalf. He'd have said she beat his time and got lost less, and she'd have said Wiggins wasn't even in the same county, and he'd have done the bark.`,
    `She didn't feel bad anymore. She sat a while longer in the cold cockpit and didn't think about much.`,
    `Then she took Danny's E6B out of the side pocket and looked at the wind side. His pencil dot, with the little circle around it. She'd worked every problem for two weeks around it, and she'd thought more than once about rubbing it out with her thumb. She put it back in the pocket the way it was.`,
    ((s.flags.thinfuel||G.thinfuel)
      ?`Walt came out of the hangar as she was leaving, with the club fuel log on a clipboard. He'd seen the Space Port receipt she'd stapled to it, or he'd seen there wasn't one at Greeley. He didn't say which. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"You landed with what you landed with,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Next time, land with what you planned to. We'll talk about it. Not today."</span> He went back inside, and she understood that was Walt being kind, and that the talk was coming.`
      :`Walt came out of the hangar as she was leaving, with the club fuel log on a clipboard and the Greeley receipt stapled to it. He looked at the fuel column, then at her. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, quiet, grudging warmth.">"That's a column I'd have signed."</span> He tucked the clipboard under his arm. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, quiet, grudging warmth.">"Eight months. Four left. I'm starting to think I'll be wrong."</span> Then, because he was Walt: <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, quiet, grudging warmth.">"Starting."</span>`),
    `That night, at 9:22, the phone buzzed on the kitchen table next to the folded chart.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="9:22 PM">${(s.score>=27)?`Saw the log. The deviation was textbook, I mean that literally, I'm putting it in my lesson notes. Next: night. Three hours, ten full stops, one cross-country over 100 miles. Then the long one, 150 and three stops, and you pick the route. Not Greeley. It smells.`:`Saw the log. We're going to talk about a few things on it, and that's what logs are for. Next: night. Three hours, ten full stops, one cross-country over 100 miles. Then the long one, 150 and three stops, and you pick the route. Not Greeley. It smells.`}</prop>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch09.mp3`, len:`about 60 minutes`},
  next:`The No-Go`
});
